iness.\",\"Loan repayment obligations.\",\"Working capital requirements.\",\"Existing business cash flow and receivables.\",\"The financial impact of a six-to-twelve-month delay in achieving projected revenue.\",\"The second location may be commercially attractive but financially premature.\",\"The distinction between profitability and cash availability is critical. A profitable business can still face serious liquidity pressure.\"]},{\"step_number\":4,\"key\":\"reframe\",\"prompt\":\"Explore other explanations and alternatives:\\n• What alternatives exist apart from expanding immediately or abandoning the opportunity?\\n• How could you test the opportunity without exposing the entire existing business to the expansion risk?\",\"insight\":\"Reframing does not mean forcing a positive interpretation. It means expanding the set of explanations or options before deciding.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What alternatives exist apart from expanding immediately or abandoning the opportunity?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. How could you test the opportunity without exposing the entire existing business to the expansion risk?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Proceed with the full expansion after securing adequate funding.\",\"Delay expansion until collections and cash reserves improve.\",\"Start with a smaller operational model.\",\"Pilot the service through a temporary or shared facility.\",\"Negotiate a phased lease or investment commitment.\",\"Validate customer demand before committing to the full capital expenditure.\",\"Set financial milestones that must be achieved before moving to the next stage.\",\"The decision can be redesigned as a staged investment rather than a single large commitment.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Proceed with the full expansion after securing adequate funding.\",\"Delay expansion until collections and cash reserves improve.\",\"Start with a smaller operational model.\",\"Pilot the service through a temporary or shared facility.\",\"Negotiate a phased lease or investment commitment.\",\"Validate customer demand before committing to the full capital expenditure.\",\"Set financial milestones that must be achieved before moving to the next stage.\",\"The decision can be redesigned as a staged investment rather than a single large commitment.\"]},{\"step_number\":5,\"key\":\"intervention\",\"prompt\":\"What would you do next, and what conditions would need to be satisfied before approving expansion?\",\"reminder\":\"There isn\'t always a perfect action. There is usually a reasonable next action based on the evidence available.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"action\",\"prompt\":\"What would you do next, and what conditions would need to be satisfied before approving expansion?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Preparing optimistic, realistic and pessimistic financial projections.\",\"Establishing a minimum cash reserve for the existing business.\",\"Calculating the break-even point of the new location.\",\"Validating demand through actual customer commitments or a pilot.\",\"Setting a maximum acceptable debt level.\",\"Defining clear performance milestones and an exit condition.\",\"Expansion should proceed only when the organisation has both a credible business opportunity and the financial capacity to execute it.\"],\"reference_example\":\"Preparing optimistic, realistic and pessimistic financial projections.\\nEstablishing a minimum cash reserve for the existing business.\\nCalculating the break-even point of the new location.\\nValidating demand through actual customer commitments or a pilot.\\nSetting a maximum acceptable debt level.\\nDefining clear performance milestones and an exit condition.\\nExpansion should proceed only when the organisation has both a credible business opportunity and the financial capacity to execute it.\"},{\"step_number\":6,\"key\":\"internalize\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"principle\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":\"Growth should strengthen the business, not put its existing stability at risk.\",\"reference_example\":\"Growth should strengthen the business, not put its existing stability at risk.\"}],\"closing_reflection\":{\"prompt\":\"If the new location takes twice as long as expected to become profitable, would your business still be able to survive comfortably?\",\"journal_input\":{\"field_key\":\"journal\",\"prompt\":\"Optional reflection entry — take a moment to reflect on today\'s scenario.\",\"required\":false}}}', `updated_at` = '2026-11-10 12:00:00';

INSERT INTO `foundation_days` (`day_number`, `phase`, `title`, `domain`, `primary_trap`, `secondary_trap`, `content_bundle`, `created_at`, `updated_at`)
VALUES (44, 3, 'The Unequal Partnership', 'Business / Partnerships', 'Emotional Reasoning', 'Mind Reading, All-or-Nothing Thinking, Catastrophizing', '{\"day\":44,\"phase\":3,\"title\":\"The Unequal Partnership\",\"domain\":\"Business / Partnerships\",\"mission\":\"A partnership can be equal in ownership but unequal in contribution, responsibility and risk.\",\"learning_objective\":\"Evaluate fairness, distinguish emotional loyalty from commercial accountability, and make difficult decisions about ownership and contribution.\",\"difficulty\":\"Advanced\",\"emotional_intensity\":\"High\",\"estimated_minutes\":\"6-8\",\"primary_skill\":\"Evaluate\",\"primary_trap\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"secondary_trap\":\"Mind Reading, All-or-Nothing Thinking, Catastrophizing\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Mind Reading\",\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"Catastrophizing\"],\"guidance_level\":\"Moderate to high guidance; strategic leadership, financial risk and long-term consequences.\",\"interaction_principle\":\"Think → Commit → Compare → Reflect\",\"scenario_text\":\"You started a business with two partners five years ago.\\nAll three partners initially agreed to equal ownership.\\nAt the time, the arrangement appeared reasonable because everyone was expected to contribute equally to the business.\\nOver the years, the situation has changed.\\nYou now manage the business full-time, handle customers, supervise employees and take responsibility for operational decisions.\\nOne partner contributes capital and participates in major strategic discussions but is not involved in daily operations.\\nThe third partner has gradually reduced involvement because of other professional commitments.\\nThe business has grown substantially, and its current valuation is much higher than when it started.\\nYou believe that your operational contribution has become significantly greater than that of the other partners.\\nYou have raised the issue informally, but the discussions have become uncomfortable.\\nOne partner believes that the original ownership agreement should remain unchanged because everyone accepted it at the beginning.\\nThe other partner agrees that your workload is greater but argues that the business\'s growth was possible because all three partners took the initial risk.\\nYou are considering asking for revised equity, a higher management fee or a performance-linked compensation structure.\\nHowever, pushing too hard could damage the partnership and create uncertainty for employees, customers and investors.\\nContinuing without addressing the issue could lead to frustration and disengagement.\\nYou must decide how to approach a partnership that has become unequal in practice but remains equal on paper.\",\"steps\":[{\"step_number\":1,\"key\":\"detect\",\"prompt\":\"What are the confirmed facts, and what are you assuming about the fairness of the current partnership arrangement?\",\"insight\":\"Facts describe what is known. Assumptions explain what the mind projects or fears.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"facts\",\"prompt\":\"Write only the confirmed facts.\",\"multiline\":true},{\"field_key\":\"assumptions\",\"prompt\":\"Write what you are assuming about this situation.\",\"multiline\":true}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":{\"facts\":[\"The three partners initially agreed to equal ownership.\",\"The operational responsibilities are no longer equally distributed.\",\"One partner contributes capital and strategic input.\",\"Another partner has reduced involvement.\",\"The business has grown in value.\",\"The partners have different views about how ownership and contribution should be treated.\"],\"assumptions\":[\"Greater operational contribution should automatically result in greater ownership.\",\"The other partners do not recognise your contribution.\",\"Revising the agreement will damage the relationship.\",\"The original arrangement must remain unchanged because it was agreed initially.\"]},\"reference_example\":\"Confirmed facts: The three partners initially agreed to equal ownership.; The operational responsibilities are no longer equally distributed.; One partner contributes capital and strategic input.\\nAssumptions: Greater operational contribution should automatically result in greater ownership.; The other partners do not recognise your contribution.; Revising the agreement will damage the relationship.\"},{\"step_number\":2,\"key\":\"decode\",\"prompt\":\"Which thinking trap(s) may be influencing your judgment, and why?\",\"input_type\":\"single_choice_with_multi_notes\",\"options\":[{\"key\":\"mind_reading\",\"label\":\"Mind Reading\",\"definition\":\"Assuming others\' intentions without evidence.\"},{\"key\":\"fortune_telling\",\"label\":\"Fortune Telling\",\"definition\":\"Treating an unverified prediction as certain.\"},{\"key\":\"emotional_reasoning\",\"label\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"definition\":\"Treating a feeling as proof of reality.\"},{\"key\":\"catastrophizing\",\"label\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"definition\":\"Assuming the absolute worst outcome.\"},{\"key\":\"confirmation_bias\",\"label\":\"Confirmation Bias\",\"definition\":\"Only seeing details that confirm the feared story.\"},{\"key\":\"rumination\",\"label\":\"Rumination\",\"definition\":\"Repetitive, circular overthinking of past events.\"},{\"key\":\"all_or_nothing\",\"label\":\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"definition\":\"Treating a minor gap as total failure or framing as either-or.\"},{\"key\":\"validation_seeking\",\"label\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"definition\":\"Allowing external approval to define self-worth or decisions.\"},{\"key\":\"sunk_cost_fallacy\",\"label\":\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"definition\":\"Continuing a path solely because of past unrecoverable investment.\"}],\"correct_option_key\":\"emotional_reasoning\",\"primary_trap\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Mind Reading\",\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"Catastrophizing\"],\"secondary_trap_keys\":[\"mind_reading\",\"all_or_nothing\",\"catastrophizing\"],\"explanation_reveal\":\"after_commit\",\"explanation\":\"Primary Thinking Trap: Emotional Reasoning\\nFrustration about the unequal workload may lead you to conclude that the existing ownership arrangement is necessarily unfair.\\nSecondary Thinking Traps:\\nMind Reading: Assuming that the other partners do not value your contribution.\\nAll-or-Nothing Thinking: Believing the only solutions are equal ownership or a complete restructuring of the partnership.\\nCatastrophizing: Assuming that raising the issue will inevitably destroy the partnership.\\nThe original ownership agreement may be fair in relation to the initial capital and risk, while the current compensation arrangement may need revision to reflect ongoing work.\\nThese are separate questions.\"},{\"step_number\":3,\"key\":\"reality_check\",\"prompt\":\"Answer these questions before deciding what the situation means:\\n• What evidence would help you assess each partner\'s actual contribution and responsibilities?\\n• Which parts of the current arrangement should be evaluated separately rather than treated as one issue?\",\"tip\":\"Missing information is not evidence. Identify what you would need to know before becoming more certain.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What evidence would help you assess each partner\'s actual contribution and responsibilities?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. Which parts of the current arrangement should be evaluated separately rather than treated as one issue?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Relevant considerations include:\",\"Initial capital invested by each partner.\",\"Additional capital contributed over time.\",\"Operational responsibilities and time commitment.\",\"Strategic, financial and commercial contributions.\",\"Personal guarantees and liabilities undertaken.\",\"Existing salary, drawings and profit distributions.\",\"The original partnership agreement.\",\"The business\'s future management requirements.\",\"Three different matters need to be distinguished:\",\"Ownership: Who owns what proportion of the business?\",\"Compensation: Who should be paid for the work they perform?\",\"Governance: Who has authority and accountability for decisions?\",\"A disagreement about compensation does not automatically require a change in ownership.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Relevant considerations include:\",\"Initial capital invested by each partner.\",\"Additional capital contributed over time.\",\"Operational responsibilities and time commitment.\",\"Strategic, financial and commercial contributions.\",\"Personal guarantees and liabilities undertaken.\",\"Existing salary, drawings and profit distributions.\",\"The original partnership agreement.\",\"The business\'s future management requirements.\",\"Three different matters need to be distinguished:\",\"Ownership: Who owns what proportion of the business?\",\"Compensation: Who should be paid for the work they perform?\",\"Governance: Who has authority and accountability for decisions?\",\"A disagreement about compensation does not automatically require a change in ownership.\"]},{\"step_number\":4,\"key\":\"reframe\",\"prompt\":\"Explore other explanations and alternatives:\\n• What realistic arrangements could recognise different contributions without unnecessarily damaging the partnership?\\n• What option could address the current imbalance while preserving the original ownership structure?\",\"insight\":\"Reframing does not mean forcing a positive interpretation. It means expanding the set of explanations or options before deciding.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What realistic arrangements could recognise different contributions without unnecessarily damaging the partnership?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. What option could address the current imbalance while preserving the original ownership structure?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Retain equal ownership but introduce market-based salaries for operational roles.\",\"Create performance-linked compensation for measurable responsibilities.\",\"Revise management responsibilities and decision-making authority.\",\"Negotiate a revised equity arrangement based on agreed valuation and contributions.\",\"Introduce a formal review of partner responsibilities every year.\",\"Offer a structured buyout if the partners cannot agree on a workable arrangement.\",\"An important alternative is to separate ownership from remuneration rather than treating both as the same issue.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Retain equal ownership but introduce market-based salaries for operational roles.\",\"Create performance-linked compensation for measurable responsibilities.\",\"Revise management responsibilities and decision-making authority.\",\"Negotiate a revised equity arrangement based on agreed valuation and contributions.\",\"Introduce a formal review of partner responsibilities every year.\",\"Offer a structured buyout if the partners cannot agree on a workable arrangement.\",\"An important alternative is to separate ownership from remuneration rather than treating both as the same issue.\"]},{\"step_number\":5,\"key\":\"intervention\",\"prompt\":\"How would you initiate the discussion, and what outcome would you seek?\",\"reminder\":\"There isn\'t always a perfect action. There is usually a reasonable next action based on the evidence available.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"action\",\"prompt\":\"How would you initiate the discussion, and what outcome would you seek?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"A constructive approach would be to:\",\"Prepare a factual record of each partner\'s contributions and responsibilities.\",\"Review the existing partnership agreement.\",\"Obtain professional legal and financial advice before changing ownership or compensation.\",\"Present alternative structures with their financial implications.\",\"Agree on responsibilities, authority, compensation and performance expectations.\",\"Document any revised understanding formally.\",\"The discussion should focus on building a sustainable partnership, not proving which partner has worked harder.\"],\"reference_example\":\"A constructive approach would be to:\\nPrepare a factual record of each partner\'s contributions and responsibilities.\\nReview the existing partnership agreement.\\nObtain professional legal and financial advice before changing ownership or compensation.\\nPresent alternative structures with their financial implications.\\nAgree on responsibilities, authority, compensation and performance expectations.\\nDocument any revised understanding formally.\\nThe discussion should focus on building a sustainable partnership, not proving which partner has worked harder.\"},{\"step_number\":6,\"key\":\"internalize\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"principle\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":\"Fairness requires clearly distinguishing ownership, contribution, compensation and accountability.\",\"reference_example\":\"Fairness requires clearly distinguishing ownership, contribution, compensation and accountability.\"}],\"closing_reflection\":{\"prompt\":\"Can a partnership remain fair when the original agreement stays unchanged but the contributions of its partners evolve?\",\"journal_input\":{\"field_key\":\"journal\",\"prompt\":\"Optional reflection entry — take a moment to reflect on today\'s scenario.\",\"required\":false}}}', '2026-11-10 12:00:00', '2026-11-10 12:00:00')
ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE `title` = 'The Unequal Partnership', `domain` = 'Business / Partnerships', `primary_trap` = 'Emotional Reasoning', `secondary_trap` = 'Mind Reading, All-or-Nothing Thinking, Catastrophizing', `content_bundle` = '{\"day\":44,\"phase\":3,\"title\":\"The Unequal Partnership\",\"domain\":\"Business / Partnerships\",\"mission\":\"A partnership can be equal in ownership but unequal in contribution, responsibility and risk.\",\"learning_objective\":\"Evaluate fairness, distinguish emotional loyalty from commercial accountability, and make difficult decisions about ownership and contribution.\",\"difficulty\":\"Advanced\",\"emotional_intensity\":\"High\",\"estimated_minutes\":\"6-8\",\"primary_skill\":\"Evaluate\",\"primary_trap\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"secondary_trap\":\"Mind Reading, All-or-Nothing Thinking, Catastrophizing\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Mind Reading\",\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"Catastrophizing\"],\"guidance_level\":\"Moderate to high guidance; strategic leadership, financial risk and long-term consequences.\",\"interaction_principle\":\"Think → Commit → Compare → Reflect\",\"scenario_text\":\"You started a business with two partners five years ago.\\nAll three partners initially agreed to equal ownership.\\nAt the time, the arrangement appeared reasonable because everyone was expected to contribute equally to the business.\\nOver the years, the situation has changed.\\nYou now manage the business full-time, handle customers, supervise employees and take responsibility for operational decisions.\\nOne partner contributes capital and participates in major strategic discussions but is not involved in daily operations.\\nThe third partner has gradually reduced involvement because of other professional commitments.\\nThe business has grown substantially, and its current valuation is much higher than when it started.\\nYou believe that your operational contribution has become significantly greater than that of the other partners.\\nYou have raised the issue informally, but the discussions have become uncomfortable.\\nOne partner believes that the original ownership agreement should remain unchanged because everyone accepted it at the beginning.\\nThe other partner agrees that your workload is greater but argues that the business\'s growth was possible because all three partners took the initial risk.\\nYou are considering asking for revised equity, a higher management fee or a performance-linked compensation structure.\\nHowever, pushing too hard could damage the partnership and create uncertainty for employees, customers and investors.\\nContinuing without addressing the issue could lead to frustration and disengagement.\\nYou must decide how to approach a partnership that has become unequal in practice but remains equal on paper.\",\"steps\":[{\"step_number\":1,\"key\":\"detect\",\"prompt\":\"What are the confirmed facts, and what are you assuming about the fairness of the current partnership arrangement?\",\"insight\":\"Facts describe what is known. Assumptions explain what the mind projects or fears.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"facts\",\"prompt\":\"Write only the confirmed facts.\",\"multiline\":true},{\"field_key\":\"assumptions\",\"prompt\":\"Write what you are assuming about this situation.\",\"multiline\":true}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":{\"facts\":[\"The three partners initially agreed to equal ownership.\",\"The operational responsibilities are no longer equally distributed.\",\"One partner contributes capital and strategic input.\",\"Another partner has reduced involvement.\",\"The business has grown in value.\",\"The partners have different views about how ownership and contribution should be treated.\"],\"assumptions\":[\"Greater operational contribution should automatically result in greater ownership.\",\"The other partners do not recognise your contribution.\",\"Revising the agreement will damage the relationship.\",\"The original arrangement must remain unchanged because it was agreed initially.\"]},\"reference_example\":\"Confirmed facts: The three partners initially agreed to equal ownership.; The operational responsibilities are no longer equally distributed.; One partner contributes capital and strategic input.\\nAssumptions: Greater operational contribution should automatically result in greater ownership.; The other partners do not recognise your contribution.; Revising the agreement will damage the relationship.\"},{\"step_number\":2,\"key\":\"decode\",\"prompt\":\"Which thinking trap(s) may be influencing your judgment, and why?\",\"input_type\":\"single_choice_with_multi_notes\",\"options\":[{\"key\":\"mind_reading\",\"label\":\"Mind Reading\",\"definition\":\"Assuming others\' intentions without evidence.\"},{\"key\":\"fortune_telling\",\"label\":\"Fortune Telling\",\"definition\":\"Treating an unverified prediction as certain.\"},{\"key\":\"emotional_reasoning\",\"label\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"definition\":\"Treating a feeling as proof of reality.\"},{\"key\":\"catastrophizing\",\"label\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"definition\":\"Assuming the absolute worst outcome.\"},{\"key\":\"confirmation_bias\",\"label\":\"Confirmation Bias\",\"definition\":\"Only seeing details that confirm the feared story.\"},{\"key\":\"rumination\",\"label\":\"Rumination\",\"definition\":\"Repetitive, circular overthinking of past events.\"},{\"key\":\"all_or_nothing\",\"label\":\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"definition\":\"Treating a minor gap as total failure or framing as either-or.\"},{\"key\":\"validation_seeking\",\"label\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"definition\":\"Allowing external approval to define self-worth or decisions.\"},{\"key\":\"sunk_cost_fallacy\",\"label\":\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"definition\":\"Continuing a path solely because of past unrecoverable investment.\"}],\"correct_option_key\":\"emotional_reasoning\",\"primary_trap\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Mind Reading\",\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"Catastrophizing\"],\"secondary_trap_keys\":[\"mind_reading\",\"all_or_nothing\",\"catastrophizing\"],\"explanation_reveal\":\"after_commit\",\"explanation\":\"Primary Thinking Trap: Emotional Reasoning\\nFrustration about the unequal workload may lead you to conclude that the existing ownership arrangement is necessarily unfair.\\nSecondary Thinking Traps:\\nMind Reading: Assuming that the other partners do not value your contribution.\\nAll-or-Nothing Thinking: Believing the only solutions are equal ownership or a complete restructuring of the partnership.\\nCatastrophizing: Assuming that raising the issue will inevitably destroy the partnership.\\nThe original ownership agreement may be fair in relation to the initial capital and risk, while the current compensation arrangement may need revision to reflect ongoing work.\\nThese are separate questions.\"},{\"step_number\":3,\"key\":\"reality_check\",\"prompt\":\"Answer these questions before deciding what the situation means:\\n• What evidence would help you assess each partner\'s actual contribution and responsibilities?\\n• Which parts of the current arrangement should be evaluated separately rather than treated as one issue?\",\"tip\":\"Missing information is not evidence. Identify what you would need to know before becoming more certain.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What evidence would help you assess each partner\'s actual contribution and responsibilities?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. Which parts of the current arrangement should be evaluated separately rather than treated as one issue?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Relevant considerations include:\",\"Initial capital invested by each partner.\",\"Additional capital contributed over time.\",\"Operational responsibilities and time commitment.\",\"Strategic, financial and commercial contributions.\",\"Personal guarantees and liabilities undertaken.\",\"Existing salary, drawings and profit distributions.\",\"The original partnership agreement.\",\"The business\'s future management requirements.\",\"Three different matters need to be distinguished:\",\"Ownership: Who owns what proportion of the business?\",\"Compensation: Who should be paid for the work they perform?\",\"Governance: Who has authority and accountability for decisions?\",\"A disagreement about compensation does not automatically require a change in ownership.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Relevant considerations include:\",\"Initial capital invested by each partner.\",\"Additional capital contributed over time.\",\"Operational responsibilities and time commitment.\",\"Strategic, financial and commercial contributions.\",\"Personal guarantees and liabilities undertaken.\",\"Existing salary, drawings and profit distributions.\",\"The original partnership agreement.\",\"The business\'s future management requirements.\",\"Three different matters need to be distinguished:\",\"Ownership: Who owns what proportion of the business?\",\"Compensation: Who should be paid for the work they perform?\",\"Governance: Who has authority and accountability for decisions?\",\"A disagreement about compensation does not automatically require a change in ownership.\"]},{\"step_number\":4,\"key\":\"reframe\",\"prompt\":\"Explore other explanations and alternatives:\\n• What realistic arrangements could recognise different contributions without unnecessarily damaging the partnership?\\n• What option could address the current imbalance while preserving the original ownership structure?\",\"insight\":\"Reframing does not mean forcing a positive interpretation. It means expanding the set of explanations or options before deciding.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What realistic arrangements could recognise different contributions without unnecessarily damaging the partnership?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. What option could address the current imbalance while preserving the original ownership structure?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Retain equal ownership but introduce market-based salaries for operational roles.\",\"Create performance-linked compensation for measurable responsibilities.\",\"Revise management responsibilities and decision-making authority.\",\"Negotiate a revised equity arrangement based on agreed valuation and contributions.\",\"Introduce a formal review of partner responsibilities every year.\",\"Offer a structured buyout if the partners cannot agree on a workable arrangement.\",\"An important alternative is to separate ownership from remuneration rather than treating both as the same issue.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Retain equal ownership but introduce market-based salaries for operational roles.\",\"Create performance-linked compensation for measurable responsibilities.\",\"Revise management responsibilities and decision-making authority.\",\"Negotiate a revised equity arrangement based on agreed valuation and contributions.\",\"Introduce a formal review of partner responsibilities every year.\",\"Offer a structured buyout if the partners cannot agree on a workable arrangement.\",\"An important alternative is to separate ownership from remuneration rather than treating both as the same issue.\"]},{\"step_number\":5,\"key\":\"intervention\",\"prompt\":\"How would you initiate the discussion, and what outcome would you seek?\",\"reminder\":\"There isn\'t always a perfect action. There is usually a reasonable next action based on the evidence available.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"action\",\"prompt\":\"How would you initiate the discussion, and what outcome would you seek?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"A constructive approach would be to:\",\"Prepare a factual record of each partner\'s contributions and responsibilities.\",\"Review the existing partnership agreement.\",\"Obtain professional legal and financial advice before changing ownership or compensation.\",\"Present alternative structures with their financial implications.\",\"Agree on responsibilities, authority, compensation and performance expectations.\",\"Document any revised understanding formally.\",\"The discussion should focus on building a sustainable partnership, not proving which partner has worked harder.\"],\"reference_example\":\"A constructive approach would be to:\\nPrepare a factual record of each partner\'s contributions and responsibilities.\\nReview the existing partnership agreement.\\nObtain professional legal and financial advice before changing ownership or compensation.\\nPresent alternative structures with their financial implications.\\nAgree on responsibilities, authority, compensation and performance expectations.\\nDocument any revised understanding formally.\\nThe discussion should focus on building a sustainable partnership, not proving which partner has worked harder.\"},{\"step_number\":6,\"key\":\"internalize\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"principle\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":\"Fairness requires clearly distinguishing ownership, contribution, compensation and accountability.\",\"reference_example\":\"Fairness requires clearly distinguishing ownership, contribution, compensation and accountability.\"}],\"closing_reflection\":{\"prompt\":\"Can a partnership remain fair when the original agreement stays unchanged but the contributions of its partners evolve?\",\"journal_input\":{\"field_key\":\"journal\",\"prompt\":\"Optional reflection entry — take a moment to reflect on today\'s scenario.\",\"required\":false}}}', `updated_at` = '2026-11-10 12:00:00';

INSERT INTO `foundation_days` (`day_number`, `phase`, `title`, `domain`, `primary_trap`, `secondary_trap`, `content_bundle`, `created_at`, `updated_at`)
VALUES (45, 3, 'The Family Business Succession', 'Family / Business Succession', 'Validation Seeking', 'Emotional Reasoning, All-or-Nothing Thinking, Catastrophizing', '{\"day\":45,\"phase\":3,\"title\":\"The Family Business Succession\",\"domain\":\"Family / Business Succession\",\"mission\":\"Protecting a family legacy does not always mean preserving the existing arrangement.\",\"learning_objective\":\"Separate family expectations from business requirements, evaluate competing interests and make decisions that balance continuity with long-term sustainability.\",\"difficulty\":\"Advanced\",\"emotional_intensity\":\"High\",\"estimated_minutes\":\"6-8\",\"primary_skill\":\"Evaluate\",\"primary_trap\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"secondary_trap\":\"Emotional Reasoning, All-or-Nothing Thinking, Catastrophizing\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"Catastrophizing\"],\"guidance_level\":\"Moderate to high guidance; strategic leadership, financial risk and long-term consequences.\",\"interaction_principle\":\"Think → Commit → Compare → Reflect\",\"scenario_text\":\"Your family owns a business that has operated successfully for more than 25 years.\\nYour father built the business from the ground up. Over time, it became an important source of income and a significant part of the family\'s identity.\\nYour father is now approaching retirement and wants you to take over its management.\\nYou have been working in a different profession for the last 15 years and have built a stable career.\\nYou understand the business to some extent, but you have never managed it independently.\\nYour father believes that you are the natural successor because you are part of the family and understand its values.\\nYour sibling is also interested in the business but does not want to take responsibility for its daily operations.\\nThe business currently has:\\nA loyal customer base.\\nAn experienced management team.\\nSome long-standing employees.\\nA few outdated operating systems.\\nIncreasing competition from professionally managed businesses.\\nYour father believes that the existing team can continue running the business under your supervision.\\nHowever, you are not certain whether the current business model will remain competitive over the next five years.\\nYou have three broad possibilities:\\nTake over the business and gradually modernise it.\\nAppoint a professional CEO while retaining family ownership.\\nSell the business and use the proceeds for other family or investment goals.\\nYour father strongly prefers the first option.\\nYou feel responsible for protecting his life\'s work, but you are also concerned about sacrificing your own career and taking responsibility for a business you may not be equipped to manage.\\nThe decision could affect your career, family relationships, employees and the financial future of the family.\",\"steps\":[{\"step_number\":1,\"key\":\"detect\",\"prompt\":\"What are the confirmed facts, and what are you assuming about your responsibility to continue the family business?\",\"insight\":\"Facts describe what is known. Assumptions explain what the mind projects or fears.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"facts\",\"prompt\":\"Write only the confirmed facts.\",\"multiline\":true},{\"field_key\":\"assumptions\",\"prompt\":\"Write what you are assuming about this situation.\",\"multiline\":true}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":{\"facts\":[\"The business has operated for more than 25 years.\",\"Your father wants you to take over.\",\"You have a separate career with 15 years of experience.\",\"Your sibling is interested but does not want daily operational responsibility.\",\"The business has strengths as well as modernisation requirements.\",\"Your father prefers family succession.\"],\"assumptions\":[\"You are the only person capable of protecting the family legacy.\",\"Selling the business would mean betraying your father\'s efforts.\",\"A professional CEO would not protect the family\'s interests.\",\"You must personally run the business for it to remain part of the family legacy.\"]},\"reference_example\":\"Confirmed facts: The business has operated for more than 25 years.; Your father wants you to take over.; You have a separate career with 15 years of experience.\\nAssumptions: You are the only person capable of protecting the family legacy.; Selling the business would mean betraying your father\'s efforts.; A professional CEO would not protect the family\'s interests.\"},{\"step_number\":2,\"key\":\"decode\",\"prompt\":\"Which thinking trap(s) may be influencing your judgment, and why?\",\"input_type\":\"single_choice_with_multi_notes\",\"options\":[{\"key\":\"mind_reading\",\"label\":\"Mind Reading\",\"definition\":\"Assuming others\' intentions without evidence.\"},{\"key\":\"fortune_telling\",\"label\":\"Fortune Telling\",\"definition\":\"Treating an unverified prediction as certain.\"},{\"key\":\"emotional_reasoning\",\"label\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"definition\":\"Treating a feeling as proof of reality.\"},{\"key\":\"catastrophizing\",\"label\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"definition\":\"Assuming the absolute worst outcome.\"},{\"key\":\"confirmation_bias\",\"label\":\"Confirmation Bias\",\"definition\":\"Only seeing details that confirm the feared story.\"},{\"key\":\"rumination\",\"label\":\"Rumination\",\"definition\":\"Repetitive, circular overthinking of past events.\"},{\"key\":\"all_or_nothing\",\"label\":\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"definition\":\"Treating a minor gap as total failure or framing as either-or.\"},{\"key\":\"validation_seeking\",\"label\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"definition\":\"Allowing external approval to define self-worth or decisions.\"},{\"key\":\"sunk_cost_fallacy\",\"label\":\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"definition\":\"Continuing a path solely because of past unrecoverable investment.\"}],\"correct_option_key\":\"validation_seeking\",\"primary_trap\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"Catastrophizing\"],\"secondary_trap_keys\":[\"emotional_reasoning\",\"all_or_nothing\",\"catastrophizing\"],\"explanation_reveal\":\"after_commit\",\"explanation\":\"Primary Thinking Trap: Validation Seeking\\nThe desire to fulfil your father\'s expectations and receive family approval may influence a major career and business decision.\\nSecondary Thinking Traps:\\nEmotional Reasoning: Feeling responsible for your father\'s legacy may be treated as proof that you should personally take over.\\nAll-or-Nothing Thinking: Assuming that the only choices are personally running the business or abandoning the family legacy.\\nCatastrophizing: Believing that refusing succession will inevitably damage family relationships.\"},{\"step_number\":3,\"key\":\"reality_check\",\"prompt\":\"Answer these questions before deciding what the situation means:\\n• What information would you need to evaluate the business\'s actual future potential and succession readiness?\\n• What assumptions about your family\'s expectations and the business\'s future need to be tested?\",\"tip\":\"Missing information is not evidence. Identify what you would need to know before becoming more certain.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What information would you need to evaluate the business\'s actual future potential and succession readiness?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. What assumptions about your family\'s expectations and the business\'s future need to be tested?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Current profitability, cash flow and debt.\",\"Customer retention and competitive position.\",\"The capability of the existing management team.\",\"Investment required for modernisation.\",\"The business\'s dependence on your father\'s relationships and knowledge.\",\"The financial implications of appointing a professional CEO.\",\"Your own interest, capability and willingness to manage the business.\",\"Your sibling\'s expectations and potential role.\",\"The family\'s financial requirements and long-term objectives.\",\"It is also important to establish whether your father wants you to own the business, manage it personally or simply ensure that it survives.\",\"These are different expectations and may require different solutions.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Current profitability, cash flow and debt.\",\"Customer retention and competitive position.\",\"The capability of the existing management team.\",\"Investment required for modernisation.\",\"The business\'s dependence on your father\'s relationships and knowledge.\",\"The financial implications of appointing a professional CEO.\",\"Your own interest, capability and willingness to manage the business.\",\"Your sibling\'s expectations and potential role.\",\"The family\'s financial requirements and long-term objectives.\",\"It is also important to establish whether your father wants you to own the business, manage it personally or simply ensure that it survives.\",\"These are different expectations and may require different solutions.\"]},{\"step_number\":4,\"key\":\"reframe\",\"prompt\":\"Explore other explanations and alternatives:\\n• What realistic succession arrangements could protect the family business without assuming that you must personally manage it?\\n• Which option would preserve the most value while respecting the interests of the family and the business?\",\"insight\":\"Reframing does not mean forcing a positive interpretation. It means expanding the set of explanations or options before deciding.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What realistic succession arrangements could protect the family business without assuming that you must personally manage it?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. Which option would preserve the most value while respecting the interests of the family and the business?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Take over the business and gradually build management capability.\",\"Retain family ownership while appointing a professional CEO.\",\"Join the board or take a strategic oversight role without managing daily operations.\",\"Develop a succession plan involving your sibling and existing management team.\",\"Sell part of the business to a strategic investor.\",\"Sell the business entirely if independent valuation and future prospects justify it.\",\"The family\'s legacy does not necessarily depend on who occupies the CEO\'s office.\",\"It may depend more on whether the business continues to create value and whether the family\'s interests are protected.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Take over the business and gradually build management capability.\",\"Retain family ownership while appointing a professional CEO.\",\"Join the board or take a strategic oversight role without managing daily operations.\",\"Develop a succession plan involving your sibling and existing management team.\",\"Sell part of the business to a strategic investor.\",\"Sell the business entirely if independent valuation and future prospects justify it.\",\"The family\'s legacy does not necessarily depend on who occupies the CEO\'s office.\",\"It may depend more on whether the business continues to create value and whether the family\'s interests are protected.\"]},{\"step_number\":5,\"key\":\"intervention\",\"prompt\":\"What would you do next before accepting or rejecting the succession responsibility?\",\"reminder\":\"There isn\'t always a perfect action. There is usually a reasonable next action based on the evidence available.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"action\",\"prompt\":\"What would you do next before accepting or rejecting the succession responsibility?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Conduct an independent business assessment.\",\"Understand the financial and operational health of the business.\",\"Clarify your father\'s expectations.\",\"Discuss your sibling\'s preferred involvement.\",\"Evaluate the existing management team\'s capabilities.\",\"Explore the cost and feasibility of professional management.\",\"Create a transition plan with clear responsibilities and review milestones.\",\"Avoid accepting a permanent responsibility simply to resolve an uncomfortable family conversation.\"],\"reference_example\":\"Conduct an independent business assessment.\\nUnderstand the financial and operational health of the business.\\nClarify your father\'s expectations.\\nDiscuss your sibling\'s preferred involvement.\\nEvaluate the existing management team\'s capabilities.\\nExplore the cost and feasibility of professional management.\\nCreate a transition plan with clear responsibilities and review milestones.\\nAvoid accepting a permanent responsibility simply to resolve an uncomfortable family conversation.\"},{\"step_number\":6,\"key\":\"internalize\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"principle\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":\"Honouring a legacy does not require repeating the decisions of the previous generation. It requires making thoughtful decisions that protect its value while preparing for the future.\",\"reference_example\":\"Honouring a legacy does not require repeating the decisions of the previous generation. It requires making thoughtful decisions that protect its value while preparing for the future.\"}],\"closing_reflection\":{\"prompt\":\"If the business could continue successfully without you managing it, would that change how you define your responsibility towards your family?\",\"journal_input\":{\"field_key\":\"journal\",\"prompt\":\"Optional reflection entry — take a moment to reflect on today\'s scenario.\",\"required\":false}}}', '2026-11-10 12:00:00', '2026-11-10 12:00:00')
ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE `title` = 'The Family Business Succession', `domain` = 'Family / Business Succession', `primary_trap` = 'Validation Seeking', `secondary_trap` = 'Emotional Reasoning, All-or-Nothing Thinking, Catastrophizing', `content_bundle` = '{\"day\":45,\"phase\":3,\"title\":\"The Family Business Succession\",\"domain\":\"Family / Business Succession\",\"mission\":\"Protecting a family legacy does not always mean preserving the existing arrangement.\",\"learning_objective\":\"Separate family expectations from business requirements, evaluate competing interests and make decisions that balance continuity with long-term sustainability.\",\"difficulty\":\"Advanced\",\"emotional_intensity\":\"High\",\"estimated_minutes\":\"6-8\",\"primary_skill\":\"Evaluate\",\"primary_trap\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"secondary_trap\":\"Emotional Reasoning, All-or-Nothing Thinking, Catastrophizing\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"Catastrophizing\"],\"guidance_level\":\"Moderate to high guidance; strategic leadership, financial risk and long-term consequences.\",\"interaction_principle\":\"Think → Commit → Compare → Reflect\",\"scenario_text\":\"Your family owns a business that has operated successfully for more than 25 years.\\nYour father built the business from the ground up. Over time, it became an important source of income and a significant part of the family\'s identity.\\nYour father is now approaching retirement and wants you to take over its management.\\nYou have been working in a different profession for the last 15 years and have built a stable career.\\nYou understand the business to some extent, but you have never managed it independently.\\nYour father believes that you are the natural successor because you are part of the family and understand its values.\\nYour sibling is also interested in the business but does not want to take responsibility for its daily operations.\\nThe business currently has:\\nA loyal customer base.\\nAn experienced management team.\\nSome long-standing employees.\\nA few outdated operating systems.\\nIncreasing competition from professionally managed businesses.\\nYour father believes that the existing team can continue running the business under your supervision.\\nHowever, you are not certain whether the current business model will remain competitive over the next five years.\\nYou have three broad possibilities:\\nTake over the business and gradually modernise it.\\nAppoint a professional CEO while retaining family ownership.\\nSell the business and use the proceeds for other family or investment goals.\\nYour father strongly prefers the first option.\\nYou feel responsible for protecting his life\'s work, but you are also concerned about sacrificing your own career and taking responsibility for a business you may not be equipped to manage.\\nThe decision could affect your career, family relationships, employees and the financial future of the family.\",\"steps\":[{\"step_number\":1,\"key\":\"detect\",\"prompt\":\"What are the confirmed facts, and what are you assuming about your responsibility to continue the family business?\",\"insight\":\"Facts describe what is known. Assumptions explain what the mind projects or fears.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"facts\",\"prompt\":\"Write only the confirmed facts.\",\"multiline\":true},{\"field_key\":\"assumptions\",\"prompt\":\"Write what you are assuming about this situation.\",\"multiline\":true}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":{\"facts\":[\"The business has operated for more than 25 years.\",\"Your father wants you to take over.\",\"You have a separate career with 15 years of experience.\",\"Your sibling is interested but does not want daily operational responsibility.\",\"The business has strengths as well as modernisation requirements.\",\"Your father prefers family succession.\"],\"assumptions\":[\"You are the only person capable of protecting the family legacy.\",\"Selling the business would mean betraying your father\'s efforts.\",\"A professional CEO would not protect the family\'s interests.\",\"You must personally run the business for it to remain part of the family legacy.\"]},\"reference_example\":\"Confirmed facts: The business has operated for more than 25 years.; Your father wants you to take over.; You have a separate career with 15 years of experience.\\nAssumptions: You are the only person capable of protecting the family legacy.; Selling the business would mean betraying your father\'s efforts.; A professional CEO would not protect the family\'s interests.\"},{\"step_number\":2,\"key\":\"decode\",\"prompt\":\"Which thinking trap(s) may be influencing your judgment, and why?\",\"input_type\":\"single_choice_with_multi_notes\",\"options\":[{\"key\":\"mind_reading\",\"label\":\"Mind Reading\",\"definition\":\"Assuming others\' intentions without evidence.\"},{\"key\":\"fortune_telling\",\"label\":\"Fortune Telling\",\"definition\":\"Treating an unverified prediction as certain.\"},{\"key\":\"emotional_reasoning\",\"label\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"definition\":\"Treating a feeling as proof of reality.\"},{\"key\":\"catastrophizing\",\"label\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"definition\":\"Assuming the absolute worst outcome.\"},{\"key\":\"confirmation_bias\",\"label\":\"Confirmation Bias\",\"definition\":\"Only seeing details that confirm the feared story.\"},{\"key\":\"rumination\",\"label\":\"Rumination\",\"definition\":\"Repetitive, circular overthinking of past events.\"},{\"key\":\"all_or_nothing\",\"label\":\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"definition\":\"Treating a minor gap as total failure or framing as either-or.\"},{\"key\":\"validation_seeking\",\"label\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"definition\":\"Allowing external approval to define self-worth or decisions.\"},{\"key\":\"sunk_cost_fallacy\",\"label\":\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"definition\":\"Continuing a path solely because of past unrecoverable investment.\"}],\"correct_option_key\":\"validation_seeking\",\"primary_trap\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"Catastrophizing\"],\"secondary_trap_keys\":[\"emotional_reasoning\",\"all_or_nothing\",\"catastrophizing\"],\"explanation_reveal\":\"after_commit\",\"explanation\":\"Primary Thinking Trap: Validation Seeking\\nThe desire to fulfil your father\'s expectations and receive family approval may influence a major career and business decision.\\nSecondary Thinking Traps:\\nEmotional Reasoning: Feeling responsible for your father\'s legacy may be treated as proof that you should personally take over.\\nAll-or-Nothing Thinking: Assuming that the only choices are personally running the business or abandoning the family legacy.\\nCatastrophizing: Believing that refusing succession will inevitably damage family relationships.\"},{\"step_number\":3,\"key\":\"reality_check\",\"prompt\":\"Answer these questions before deciding what the situation means:\\n• What information would you need to evaluate the business\'s actual future potential and succession readiness?\\n• What assumptions about your family\'s expectations and the business\'s future need to be tested?\",\"tip\":\"Missing information is not evidence. Identify what you would need to know before becoming more certain.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What information would you need to evaluate the business\'s actual future potential and succession readiness?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. What assumptions about your family\'s expectations and the business\'s future need to be tested?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Current profitability, cash flow and debt.\",\"Customer retention and competitive position.\",\"The capability of the existing management team.\",\"Investment required for modernisation.\",\"The business\'s dependence on your father\'s relationships and knowledge.\",\"The financial implications of appointing a professional CEO.\",\"Your own interest, capability and willingness to manage the business.\",\"Your sibling\'s expectations and potential role.\",\"The family\'s financial requirements and long-term objectives.\",\"It is also important to establish whether your father wants you to own the business, manage it personally or simply ensure that it survives.\",\"These are different expectations and may require different solutions.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Current profitability, cash flow and debt.\",\"Customer retention and competitive position.\",\"The capability of the existing management team.\",\"Investment required for modernisation.\",\"The business\'s dependence on your father\'s relationships and knowledge.\",\"The financial implications of appointing a professional CEO.\",\"Your own interest, capability and willingness to manage the business.\",\"Your sibling\'s expectations and potential role.\",\"The family\'s financial requirements and long-term objectives.\",\"It is also important to establish whether your father wants you to own the business, manage it personally or simply ensure that it survives.\",\"These are different expectations and may require different solutions.\"]},{\"step_number\":4,\"key\":\"reframe\",\"prompt\":\"Explore other explanations and alternatives:\\n• What realistic succession arrangements could protect the family business without assuming that you must personally manage it?\\n• Which option would preserve the most value while respecting the interests of the family and the business?\",\"insight\":\"Reframing does not mean forcing a positive interpretation. It means expanding the set of explanations or options before deciding.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What realistic succession arrangements could protect the family business without assuming that you must personally manage it?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. Which option would preserve the most value while respecting the interests of the family and the business?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Take over the business and gradually build management capability.\",\"Retain family ownership while appointing a professional CEO.\",\"Join the board or take a strategic oversight role without managing daily operations.\",\"Develop a succession plan involving your sibling and existing management team.\",\"Sell part of the business to a strategic investor.\",\"Sell the business entirely if independent valuation and future prospects justify it.\",\"The family\'s legacy does not necessarily depend on who occupies the CEO\'s office.\",\"It may depend more on whether the business continues to create value and whether the family\'s interests are protected.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Take over the business and gradually build management capability.\",\"Retain family ownership while appointing a professional CEO.\",\"Join the board or take a strategic oversight role without managing daily operations.\",\"Develop a succession plan involving your sibling and existing management team.\",\"Sell part of the business to a strategic investor.\",\"Sell the business entirely if independent valuation and future prospects justify it.\",\"The family\'s legacy does not necessarily depend on who occupies the CEO\'s office.\",\"It may depend more on whether the business continues to create value and whether the family\'s interests are protected.\"]},{\"step_number\":5,\"key\":\"intervention\",\"prompt\":\"What would you do next before accepting or rejecting the succession responsibility?\",\"reminder\":\"There isn\'t always a perfect action. There is usually a reasonable next action based on the evidence available.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"action\",\"prompt\":\"What would you do next before accepting or rejecting the succession responsibility?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Conduct an independent business assessment.\",\"Understand the financial and operational health of the business.\",\"Clarify your father\'s expectations.\",\"Discuss your sibling\'s preferred involvement.\",\"Evaluate the existing management team\'s capabilities.\",\"Explore the cost and feasibility of professional management.\",\"Create a transition plan with clear responsibilities and review milestones.\",\"Avoid accepting a permanent responsibility simply to resolve an uncomfortable family conversation.\"],\"reference_example\":\"Conduct an independent business assessment.\\nUnderstand the financial and operational health of the business.\\nClarify your father\'s expectations.\\nDiscuss your sibling\'s preferred involvement.\\nEvaluate the existing management team\'s capabilities.\\nExplore the cost and feasibility of professional management.\\nCreate a transition plan with clear responsibilities and review milestones.\\nAvoid accepting a permanent responsibility simply to resolve an uncomfortable family conversation.\"},{\"step_number\":6,\"key\":\"internalize\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"principle\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":\"Honouring a legacy does not require repeating the decisions of the previous generation. It requires making thoughtful decisions that protect its value while preparing for the future.\",\"reference_example\":\"Honouring a legacy does not require repeating the decisions of the previous generation. It requires making thoughtful decisions that protect its value while preparing for the future.\"}],\"closing_reflection\":{\"prompt\":\"If the business could continue successfully without you managing it, would that change how you define your responsibility towards your family?\",\"journal_input\":{\"field_key\":\"journal\",\"prompt\":\"Optional reflection entry — take a moment to reflect on today\'s scenario.\",\"required\":false}}}', `updated_at` = '2026-11-10 12:00:00';

INSERT INTO `foundation_days` (`day_number`, `phase`, `title`, `domain`, `primary_trap`, `secondary_trap`, `content_bundle`, `created_at`, `updated_at`)
VALUES (46, 3, 'The Customer Who Contributes 40% of Your Revenue', 'Entrepreneurship / Customer Concentration Risk', 'Catastrophizing', 'Fortune Telling, Validation Seeking, All-or-Nothing Thinking', '{\"day\":46,\"phase\":3,\"title\":\"The Customer Who Contributes 40% of Your Revenue\",\"domain\":\"Entrepreneurship / Customer Concentration Risk\",\"mission\":\"Business growth should not come at the cost of becoming dependent on a single customer.\",\"learning_objective\":\"Recognise customer concentration risk, distinguish revenue from business stability, and evaluate difficult commercial relationships.\",\"difficulty\":\"Advanced\",\"emotional_intensity\":\"High\",\"estimated_minutes\":\"6-8\",\"primary_skill\":\"Evaluate\",\"primary_trap\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"secondary_trap\":\"Fortune Telling, Validation Seeking, All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Fortune Telling\",\"Validation Seeking\",\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\"],\"guidance_level\":\"Moderate to high guidance; strategic leadership, financial risk and long-term consequences.\",\"interaction_principle\":\"Think → Commit → Compare → Reflect\",\"scenario_text\":\"You own a growing B2B services company that has been operating for six years.\\nThe business generates approximately ₹50 lakh in monthly revenue. One large corporate customer contributes nearly 40% of that revenue.\\nThe relationship has been commercially valuable. Payments have generally been regular, and the customer has helped establish your company\'s credibility in the market.\\nHowever, the customer has recently started demanding:\\nA 15% reduction in service charges.\\nLonger payment terms, extending from 30 to 90 days.\\nAdditional services without corresponding increases in fees.\\nGreater operational flexibility without committing to minimum volumes.\\nYour sales head believes the relationship must be protected at any cost because losing this customer would significantly affect revenue.\\nYour finance head disagrees. The customer\'s payment delays have already increased working capital pressure, and the proposed price reduction could affect profitability.\\nYou have other customers, but replacing this business would take time.\\nThe customer has informally indicated that it may shift its contract to a competitor if you do not agree to the revised terms.\\nYou must decide whether to accept the demands, negotiate, reduce your dependence or walk away.\",\"steps\":[{\"step_number\":1,\"key\":\"detect\",\"prompt\":\"What are the confirmed facts, and what are you assuming about the consequences of losing this customer?\",\"insight\":\"Facts describe what is known. Assumptions explain what the mind projects or fears.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"facts\",\"prompt\":\"Write only the confirmed facts.\",\"multiline\":true},{\"field_key\":\"assumptions\",\"prompt\":\"Write what you are assuming about this situation.\",\"multiline\":true}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":{\"facts\":[\"Monthly revenue is approximately ₹50 lakh.\",\"One customer contributes 40% of revenue.\",\"The customer is demanding lower prices and longer payment terms.\",\"Other customers exist but cannot immediately replace the revenue.\",\"The proposed terms could affect profitability and cash flow.\"],\"assumptions\":[\"Losing the customer would necessarily threaten the business.\",\"Accepting the demands will preserve the relationship.\",\"The customer has no viable alternative suppliers.\",\"Revenue retention is more important than profitability.\",\"The important distinction is between retaining revenue and retaining economically valuable business.\"]},\"reference_example\":\"Confirmed facts: Monthly revenue is approximately ₹50 lakh.; One customer contributes 40% of revenue.; The customer is demanding lower prices and longer payment terms.\\nAssumptions: Losing the customer would necessarily threaten the business.; Accepting the demands will preserve the relationship.; The customer has no viable alternative suppliers.\"},{\"step_number\":2,\"key\":\"decode\",\"prompt\":\"Which thinking trap(s) may be influencing your judgment, and why?\",\"input_type\":\"single_choice_with_multi_notes\",\"options\":[{\"key\":\"mind_reading\",\"label\":\"Mind Reading\",\"definition\":\"Assuming others\' intentions without evidence.\"},{\"key\":\"fortune_telling\",\"label\":\"Fortune Telling\",\"definition\":\"Treating an unverified prediction as certain.\"},{\"key\":\"emotional_reasoning\",\"label\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"definition\":\"Treating a feeling as proof of reality.\"},{\"key\":\"catastrophizing\",\"label\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"definition\":\"Assuming the absolute worst outcome.\"},{\"key\":\"confirmation_bias\",\"label\":\"Confirmation Bias\",\"definition\":\"Only seeing details that confirm the feared story.\"},{\"key\":\"rumination\",\"label\":\"Rumination\",\"definition\":\"Repetitive, circular overthinking of past events.\"},{\"key\":\"all_or_nothing\",\"label\":\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"definition\":\"Treating a minor gap as total failure or framing as either-or.\"},{\"key\":\"validation_seeking\",\"label\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"definition\":\"Allowing external approval to define self-worth or decisions.\"},{\"key\":\"sunk_cost_fallacy\",\"label\":\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"definition\":\"Continuing a path solely because of past unrecoverable investment.\"}],\"correct_option_key\":\"catastrophizing\",\"primary_trap\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Fortune Telling\",\"Validation Seeking\",\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\"],\"secondary_trap_keys\":[\"fortune_telling\",\"validation_seeking\",\"all_or_nothing\"],\"explanation_reveal\":\"after_commit\",\"explanation\":\"Primary Thinking Trap: Catastrophizing\\nThe business owner may exaggerate the consequences of losing one customer and therefore accept commercially unfavourable conditions.\\nSecondary Thinking Traps:\\nFortune Telling: Assuming that losing the customer will lead to business failure.\\nValidation Seeking: Prioritising the customer\'s approval over the company\'s commercial interests.\\nAll-or-Nothing Thinking: Believing the only options are accepting every demand or losing the entire contract.\\nOther interpretations may also be defensible.\"},{\"step_number\":3,\"key\":\"reality_check\",\"prompt\":\"Answer these questions before deciding what the situation means:\\n• What financial and commercial information would help you evaluate whether this customer remains worth retaining?\\n• What would happen to the business if this customer reduced its orders or stopped working with you?\",\"tip\":\"Missing information is not evidence. Identify what you would need to know before becoming more certain.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What financial and commercial information would help you evaluate whether this customer remains worth retaining?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. What would happen to the business if this customer reduced its orders or stopped working with you?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Actual contribution margin from this customer.\",\"Cost of servicing the account.\",\"Outstanding receivables and payment history.\",\"Cost of replacing the business.\",\"Available capacity to acquire new customers.\",\"Impact of revised prices on profitability.\",\"Contractual termination provisions.\",\"Customer acquisition pipeline.\",\"A customer contributing 40% of revenue may not contribute 40% of profit.\",\"The business must evaluate economic contribution, not just revenue concentration.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Actual contribution margin from this customer.\",\"Cost of servicing the account.\",\"Outstanding receivables and payment history.\",\"Cost of replacing the business.\",\"Available capacity to acquire new customers.\",\"Impact of revised prices on profitability.\",\"Contractual termination provisions.\",\"Customer acquisition pipeline.\",\"A customer contributing 40% of revenue may not contribute 40% of profit.\",\"The business must evaluate economic contribution, not just revenue concentration.\"]},{\"step_number\":4,\"key\":\"reframe\",\"prompt\":\"Explore other explanations and alternatives:\\n• What alternatives exist beyond accepting all the demands or terminating the relationship?\\n• How could you restructure the commercial arrangement to reduce dependence while protecting profitability?\",\"insight\":\"Reframing does not mean forcing a positive interpretation. It means expanding the set of explanations or options before deciding.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What alternatives exist beyond accepting all the demands or terminating the relationship?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. How could you restructure the commercial arrangement to reduce dependence while protecting profitability?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Negotiate price reductions against guaranteed minimum volumes.\",\"Offer different service packages at different price points.\",\"Link extended payment terms to revised commercial conditions.\",\"Introduce a phased reduction in customer concentration.\",\"Develop additional customer acquisition channels.\",\"Retain only profitable portions of the contract.\",\"Establish a formal credit limit and payment discipline.\",\"The objective is to create a commercially sustainable relationship, not merely preserve an existing one.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Negotiate price reductions against guaranteed minimum volumes.\",\"Offer different service packages at different price points.\",\"Link extended payment terms to revised commercial conditions.\",\"Introduce a phased reduction in customer concentration.\",\"Develop additional customer acquisition channels.\",\"Retain only profitable portions of the contract.\",\"Establish a formal credit limit and payment discipline.\",\"The objective is to create a commercially sustainable relationship, not merely preserve an existing one.\"]},{\"step_number\":5,\"key\":\"intervention\",\"prompt\":\"What would you do next, and what conditions would you establish before agreeing to revised terms?\",\"reminder\":\"There isn\'t always a perfect action. There is usually a reasonable next action based on the evidence available.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"action\",\"prompt\":\"What would you do next, and what conditions would you establish before agreeing to revised terms?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Calculating customer-level profitability.\",\"Modelling the financial impact of the proposed demands.\",\"Negotiating minimum business commitments.\",\"Establishing payment and credit conditions.\",\"Preparing a contingency plan if the customer exits.\",\"Setting measurable customer diversification targets.\",\"Do not allow one customer\'s commercial importance to become control over your entire business.\"],\"reference_example\":\"Calculating customer-level profitability.\\nModelling the financial impact of the proposed demands.\\nNegotiating minimum business commitments.\\nEstablishing payment and credit conditions.\\nPreparing a contingency plan if the customer exits.\\nSetting measurable customer diversification targets.\\nDo not allow one customer\'s commercial importance to become control over your entire business.\"},{\"step_number\":6,\"key\":\"internalize\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"principle\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":\"Revenue concentration can create hidden business vulnerability. A good entrepreneur builds customer relationships without allowing any single customer to determine the company\'s financial survival.\",\"reference_example\":\"Revenue concentration can create hidden business vulnerability. A good entrepreneur builds customer relationships without allowing any single customer to determine the company\'s financial survival.\"}],\"closing_reflection\":{\"prompt\":\"If retaining a large customer requires sacrificing profitability and financial independence, is retaining that customer necessarily good business?\",\"journal_input\":{\"field_key\":\"journal\",\"prompt\":\"Optional reflection entry — take a moment to reflect on today\'s scenario.\",\"required\":false}}}', '2026-11-10 12:00:00', '2026-11-10 12:00:00')
ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE `title` = 'The Customer Who Contributes 40% of Your Revenue', `domain` = 'Entrepreneurship / Customer Concentration Risk', `primary_trap` = 'Catastrophizing', `secondary_trap` = 'Fortune Telling, Validation Seeking, All-or-Nothing Thinking', `content_bundle` = '{\"day\":46,\"phase\":3,\"title\":\"The Customer Who Contributes 40% of Your Revenue\",\"domain\":\"Entrepreneurship / Customer Concentration Risk\",\"mission\":\"Business growth should not come at the cost of becoming dependent on a single customer.\",\"learning_objective\":\"Recognise customer concentration risk, distinguish revenue from business stability, and evaluate difficult commercial relationships.\",\"difficulty\":\"Advanced\",\"emotional_intensity\":\"High\",\"estimated_minutes\":\"6-8\",\"primary_skill\":\"Evaluate\",\"primary_trap\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"secondary_trap\":\"Fortune Telling, Validation Seeking, All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Fortune Telling\",\"Validation Seeking\",\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\"],\"guidance_level\":\"Moderate to high guidance; strategic leadership, financial risk and long-term consequences.\",\"interaction_principle\":\"Think → Commit → Compare → Reflect\",\"scenario_text\":\"You own a growing B2B services company that has been operating for six years.\\nThe business generates approximately ₹50 lakh in monthly revenue. One large corporate customer contributes nearly 40% of that revenue.\\nThe relationship has been commercially valuable. Payments have generally been regular, and the customer has helped establish your company\'s credibility in the market.\\nHowever, the customer has recently started demanding:\\nA 15% reduction in service charges.\\nLonger payment terms, extending from 30 to 90 days.\\nAdditional services without corresponding increases in fees.\\nGreater operational flexibility without committing to minimum volumes.\\nYour sales head believes the relationship must be protected at any cost because losing this customer would significantly affect revenue.\\nYour finance head disagrees. The customer\'s payment delays have already increased working capital pressure, and the proposed price reduction could affect profitability.\\nYou have other customers, but replacing this business would take time.\\nThe customer has informally indicated that it may shift its contract to a competitor if you do not agree to the revised terms.\\nYou must decide whether to accept the demands, negotiate, reduce your dependence or walk away.\",\"steps\":[{\"step_number\":1,\"key\":\"detect\",\"prompt\":\"What are the confirmed facts, and what are you assuming about the consequences of losing this customer?\",\"insight\":\"Facts describe what is known. Assumptions explain what the mind projects or fears.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"facts\",\"prompt\":\"Write only the confirmed facts.\",\"multiline\":true},{\"field_key\":\"assumptions\",\"prompt\":\"Write what you are assuming about this situation.\",\"multiline\":true}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":{\"facts\":[\"Monthly revenue is approximately ₹50 lakh.\",\"One customer contributes 40% of revenue.\",\"The customer is demanding lower prices and longer payment terms.\",\"Other customers exist but cannot immediately replace the revenue.\",\"The proposed terms could affect profitability and cash flow.\"],\"assumptions\":[\"Losing the customer would necessarily threaten the business.\",\"Accepting the demands will preserve the relationship.\",\"The customer has no viable alternative suppliers.\",\"Revenue retention is more important than profitability.\",\"The important distinction is between retaining revenue and retaining economically valuable business.\"]},\"reference_example\":\"Confirmed facts: Monthly revenue is approximately ₹50 lakh.; One customer contributes 40% of revenue.; The customer is demanding lower prices and longer payment terms.\\nAssumptions: Losing the customer would necessarily threaten the business.; Accepting the demands will preserve the relationship.; The customer has no viable alternative suppliers.\"},{\"step_number\":2,\"key\":\"decode\",\"prompt\":\"Which thinking trap(s) may be influencing your judgment, and why?\",\"input_type\":\"single_choice_with_multi_notes\",\"options\":[{\"key\":\"mind_reading\",\"label\":\"Mind Reading\",\"definition\":\"Assuming others\' intentions without evidence.\"},{\"key\":\"fortune_telling\",\"label\":\"Fortune Telling\",\"definition\":\"Treating an unverified prediction as certain.\"},{\"key\":\"emotional_reasoning\",\"label\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"definition\":\"Treating a feeling as proof of reality.\"},{\"key\":\"catastrophizing\",\"label\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"definition\":\"Assuming the absolute worst outcome.\"},{\"key\":\"confirmation_bias\",\"label\":\"Confirmation Bias\",\"definition\":\"Only seeing details that confirm the feared story.\"},{\"key\":\"rumination\",\"label\":\"Rumination\",\"definition\":\"Repetitive, circular overthinking of past events.\"},{\"key\":\"all_or_nothing\",\"label\":\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"definition\":\"Treating a minor gap as total failure or framing as either-or.\"},{\"key\":\"validation_seeking\",\"label\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"definition\":\"Allowing external approval to define self-worth or decisions.\"},{\"key\":\"sunk_cost_fallacy\",\"label\":\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"definition\":\"Continuing a path solely because of past unrecoverable investment.\"}],\"correct_option_key\":\"catastrophizing\",\"primary_trap\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Fortune Telling\",\"Validation Seeking\",\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\"],\"secondary_trap_keys\":[\"fortune_telling\",\"validation_seeking\",\"all_or_nothing\"],\"explanation_reveal\":\"after_commit\",\"explanation\":\"Primary Thinking Trap: Catastrophizing\\nThe business owner may exaggerate the consequences of losing one customer and therefore accept commercially unfavourable conditions.\\nSecondary Thinking Traps:\\nFortune Telling: Assuming that losing the customer will lead to business failure.\\nValidation Seeking: Prioritising the customer\'s approval over the company\'s commercial interests.\\nAll-or-Nothing Thinking: Believing the only options are accepting every demand or losing the entire contract.\\nOther interpretations may also be defensible.\"},{\"step_number\":3,\"key\":\"reality_check\",\"prompt\":\"Answer these questions before deciding what the situation means:\\n• What financial and commercial information would help you evaluate whether this customer remains worth retaining?\\n• What would happen to the business if this customer reduced its orders or stopped working with you?\",\"tip\":\"Missing information is not evidence. Identify what you would need to know before becoming more certain.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What financial and commercial information would help you evaluate whether this customer remains worth retaining?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. What would happen to the business if this customer reduced its orders or stopped working with you?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Actual contribution margin from this customer.\",\"Cost of servicing the account.\",\"Outstanding receivables and payment history.\",\"Cost of replacing the business.\",\"Available capacity to acquire new customers.\",\"Impact of revised prices on profitability.\",\"Contractual termination provisions.\",\"Customer acquisition pipeline.\",\"A customer contributing 40% of revenue may not contribute 40% of profit.\",\"The business must evaluate economic contribution, not just revenue concentration.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Actual contribution margin from this customer.\",\"Cost of servicing the account.\",\"Outstanding receivables and payment history.\",\"Cost of replacing the business.\",\"Available capacity to acquire new customers.\",\"Impact of revised prices on profitability.\",\"Contractual termination provisions.\",\"Customer acquisition pipeline.\",\"A customer contributing 40% of revenue may not contribute 40% of profit.\",\"The business must evaluate economic contribution, not just revenue concentration.\"]},{\"step_number\":4,\"key\":\"reframe\",\"prompt\":\"Explore other explanations and alternatives:\\n• What alternatives exist beyond accepting all the demands or terminating the relationship?\\n• How could you restructure the commercial arrangement to reduce dependence while protecting profitability?\",\"insight\":\"Reframing does not mean forcing a positive interpretation. It means expanding the set of explanations or options before deciding.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What alternatives exist beyond accepting all the demands or terminating the relationship?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. How could you restructure the commercial arrangement to reduce dependence while protecting profitability?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Negotiate price reductions against guaranteed minimum volumes.\",\"Offer different service packages at different price points.\",\"Link extended payment terms to revised commercial conditions.\",\"Introduce a phased reduction in customer concentration.\",\"Develop additional customer acquisition channels.\",\"Retain only profitable portions of the contract.\",\"Establish a formal credit limit and payment discipline.\",\"The objective is to create a commercially sustainable relationship, not merely preserve an existing one.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Negotiate price reductions against guaranteed minimum volumes.\",\"Offer different service packages at different price points.\",\"Link extended payment terms to revised commercial conditions.\",\"Introduce a phased reduction in customer concentration.\",\"Develop additional customer acquisition channels.\",\"Retain only profitable portions of the contract.\",\"Establish a formal credit limit and payment discipline.\",\"The objective is to create a commercially sustainable relationship, not merely preserve an existing one.\"]},{\"step_number\":5,\"key\":\"intervention\",\"prompt\":\"What would you do next, and what conditions would you establish before agreeing to revised terms?\",\"reminder\":\"There isn\'t always a perfect action. There is usually a reasonable next action based on the evidence available.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"action\",\"prompt\":\"What would you do next, and what conditions would you establish before agreeing to revised terms?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Calculating customer-level profitability.\",\"Modelling the financial impact of the proposed demands.\",\"Negotiating minimum business commitments.\",\"Establishing payment and credit conditions.\",\"Preparing a contingency plan if the customer exits.\",\"Setting measurable customer diversification targets.\",\"Do not allow one customer\'s commercial importance to become control over your entire business.\"],\"reference_example\":\"Calculating customer-level profitability.\\nModelling the financial impact of the proposed demands.\\nNegotiating minimum business commitments.\\nEstablishing payment and credit conditions.\\nPreparing a contingency plan if the customer exits.\\nSetting measurable customer diversification targets.\\nDo not allow one customer\'s commercial importance to become control over your entire business.\"},{\"step_number\":6,\"key\":\"internalize\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"principle\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":\"Revenue concentration can create hidden business vulnerability. A good entrepreneur builds customer relationships without allowing any single customer to determine the company\'s financial survival.\",\"reference_example\":\"Revenue concentration can create hidden business vulnerability. A good entrepreneur builds customer relationships without allowing any single customer to determine the company\'s financial survival.\"}],\"closing_reflection\":{\"prompt\":\"If retaining a large customer requires sacrificing profitability and financial independence, is retaining that customer necessarily good business?\",\"journal_input\":{\"field_key\":\"journal\",\"prompt\":\"Optional reflection entry — take a moment to reflect on today\'s scenario.\",\"required\":false}}}', `updated_at` = '2026-11-10 12:00:00';

INSERT INTO `foundation_days` (`day_number`, `phase`, `title`, `domain`, `primary_trap`, `secondary_trap`, `content_bundle`, `created_at`, `updated_at`)
VALUES (47, 3, 'The Star Employee Who Is Damaging Your Organisation', 'Workplace / People Management', 'Confirmation Bias', 'Sunk Cost Fallacy, Catastrophizing, Emotional Reasoning', '{\"day\":47,\"phase\":3,\"title\":\"The Star Employee Who Is Damaging Your Organisation\",\"domain\":\"Workplace / People Management\",\"mission\":\"Individual performance cannot justify behaviour that damages the larger organisation.\",\"learning_objective\":\"Balance individual performance against organisational culture, recognise the cost of tolerating toxic behaviour and make objective people-management decisions.\",\"difficulty\":\"Advanced\",\"emotional_intensity\":\"High\",\"estimated_minutes\":\"6-8\",\"primary_skill\":\"Evaluate\",\"primary_trap\":\"Confirmation Bias\",\"secondary_trap\":\"Sunk Cost Fallacy, Catastrophizing, Emotional Reasoning\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"Catastrophizing\",\"Emotional Reasoning\"],\"guidance_level\":\"Moderate to high guidance; strategic leadership, financial risk and long-term consequences.\",\"interaction_principle\":\"Think → Commit → Compare → Reflect\",\"scenario_text\":\"You are the CEO of a growing company with approximately 200 employees.\\nYour sales head has been with the organisation for five years and consistently delivers outstanding results. He contributes nearly 30% of the company\'s new business.\\nHowever, several concerns have emerged:\\nHe regularly speaks disrespectfully to junior employees.\\nTwo capable employees have resigned after conflicts with him.\\nHe bypasses established reporting structures.\\nHe frequently takes credit for the work of his team.\\nOther department heads have complained about his behaviour.\\nHR has documented some incidents, but no formal disciplinary action has been taken.\\nYour management team is divided.\\nThe business head believes that losing him could affect revenue significantly. HR believes that continuing to tolerate his behaviour will damage employee morale and organisational culture.\\nThe sales head himself argues that his results justify his unconventional management style.\\nYou must decide whether to coach him, formally discipline him, change his responsibilities or terminate his employment.\",\"steps\":[{\"step_number\":1,\"key\":\"detect\",\"prompt\":\"What are the confirmed facts, and what are you assuming about the consequences of taking action against this employee?\",\"insight\":\"Facts describe what is known. Assumptions explain what the mind projects or fears.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"facts\",\"prompt\":\"Write only the confirmed facts.\",\"multiline\":true},{\"field_key\":\"assumptions\",\"prompt\":\"Write what you are assuming about this situation.\",\"multiline\":true}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":{\"facts\":[\"The employee delivers strong sales performance.\",\"Multiple behavioural complaints have been reported.\",\"Some employees have resigned following conflicts.\",\"HR has documented concerns.\",\"Management is divided about the appropriate response.\"],\"assumptions\":[\"The employee is indispensable.\",\"High performers deserve greater behavioural flexibility.\",\"Disciplinary action will damage business performance.\",\"Employees will continue tolerating the situation.\",\"Performance is one dimension of an employee\'s contribution. Behaviour and its effect on the organisation are also relevant.\"]},\"reference_example\":\"Confirmed facts: The employee delivers strong sales performance.; Multiple behavioural complaints have been reported.; Some employees have resigned following conflicts.\\nAssumptions: The employee is indispensable.; High performers deserve greater behavioural flexibility.; Disciplinary action will damage business performance.\"},{\"step_number\":2,\"key\":\"decode\",\"prompt\":\"Which thinking trap(s) may be influencing your judgment, and why?\",\"input_type\":\"single_choice_with_multi_notes\",\"options\":[{\"key\":\"mind_reading\",\"label\":\"Mind Reading\",\"definition\":\"Assuming others\' intentions without evidence.\"},{\"key\":\"fortune_telling\",\"label\":\"Fortune Telling\",\"definition\":\"Treating an unverified prediction as certain.\"},{\"key\":\"emotional_reasoning\",\"label\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"definition\":\"Treating a feeling as proof of reality.\"},{\"key\":\"catastrophizing\",\"label\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"definition\":\"Assuming the absolute worst outcome.\"},{\"key\":\"confirmation_bias\",\"label\":\"Confirmation Bias\",\"definition\":\"Only seeing details that confirm the feared story.\"},{\"key\":\"rumination\",\"label\":\"Rumination\",\"definition\":\"Repetitive, circular overthinking of past events.\"},{\"key\":\"all_or_nothing\",\"label\":\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"definition\":\"Treating a minor gap as total failure or framing as either-or.\"},{\"key\":\"validation_seeking\",\"label\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"definition\":\"Allowing external approval to define self-worth or decisions.\"},{\"key\":\"sunk_cost_fallacy\",\"label\":\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"definition\":\"Continuing a path solely because of past unrecoverable investment.\"}],\"correct_option_key\":\"confirmation_bias\",\"primary_trap\":\"Confirmation Bias\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"Catastrophizing\",\"Emotional Reasoning\"],\"secondary_trap_keys\":[\"sunk_cost_fallacy\",\"catastrophizing\",\"emotional_reasoning\"],\"explanation_reveal\":\"after_commit\",\"explanation\":\"Primary Thinking Trap: Confirmation Bias\\nManagement may give disproportionate importance to sales numbers while overlooking evidence of cultural and operational damage.\\nSecondary Thinking Traps:\\nSunk Cost Fallacy: Protecting the employee because of his long service and previous contribution.\\nCatastrophizing: Assuming that termination will cause serious business disruption.\\nEmotional Reasoning: Allowing admiration for his performance to influence judgment about his behaviour.\"},{\"step_number\":3,\"key\":\"reality_check\",\"prompt\":\"Answer these questions before deciding what the situation means:\\n• What evidence should be examined before deciding the employee\'s future?\\n• How would you measure the actual organisational cost of retaining this employee?\",\"tip\":\"Missing information is not evidence. Identify what you would need to know before becoming more certain.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What evidence should be examined before deciding the employee\'s future?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. How would you measure the actual organisational cost of retaining this employee?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Documented behavioural incidents.\",\"Employee attrition and exit interview findings.\",\"Team productivity and engagement.\",\"Customer feedback.\",\"Sales performance and profitability.\",\"Previous counselling or disciplinary records.\",\"The availability of succession or replacement options.\",\"Applicable employment policies and legal requirements.\",\"A high-performing individual can still create a net organisational loss if the wider damage exceeds the value of their contribution.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Documented behavioural incidents.\",\"Employee attrition and exit interview findings.\",\"Team productivity and engagement.\",\"Customer feedback.\",\"Sales performance and profitability.\",\"Previous counselling or disciplinary records.\",\"The availability of succession or replacement options.\",\"Applicable employment policies and legal requirements.\",\"A high-performing individual can still create a net organisational loss if the wider damage exceeds the value of their contribution.\"]},{\"step_number\":4,\"key\":\"reframe\",\"prompt\":\"Explore other explanations and alternatives:\\n• What alternatives exist apart from ignoring the behaviour or immediately terminating the employee?\\n• How could you protect the team while giving the employee a fair opportunity to improve?\",\"insight\":\"Reframing does not mean forcing a positive interpretation. It means expanding the set of explanations or options before deciding.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What alternatives exist apart from ignoring the behaviour or immediately terminating the employee?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. How could you protect the team while giving the employee a fair opportunity to improve?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Formal performance and behaviour improvement plan.\",\"Documented counselling with measurable expectations.\",\"Leadership coaching.\",\"Removal of people-management responsibilities.\",\"Changes to reporting structures.\",\"Formal disciplinary proceedings where justified.\",\"Termination if misconduct continues or is sufficiently serious.\",\"Performance and behavioural expectations should apply consistently across organisational levels.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Formal performance and behaviour improvement plan.\",\"Documented counselling with measurable expectations.\",\"Leadership coaching.\",\"Removal of people-management responsibilities.\",\"Changes to reporting structures.\",\"Formal disciplinary proceedings where justified.\",\"Termination if misconduct continues or is sufficiently serious.\",\"Performance and behavioural expectations should apply consistently across organisational levels.\"]},{\"step_number\":5,\"key\":\"intervention\",\"prompt\":\"What action would you take, and what measurable expectations would you communicate?\",\"reminder\":\"There isn\'t always a perfect action. There is usually a reasonable next action based on the evidence available.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"action\",\"prompt\":\"What action would you take, and what measurable expectations would you communicate?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Review all documented complaints.\",\"Conduct a fair investigation.\",\"Meet the employee formally.\",\"Communicate specific behavioural expectations.\",\"Define a review period and consequences.\",\"Protect employees from retaliation.\",\"Prepare a succession or business-continuity plan.\",\"Do not confuse protecting a valuable employee with protecting unacceptable behaviour.\"],\"reference_example\":\"Review all documented complaints.\\nConduct a fair investigation.\\nMeet the employee formally.\\nCommunicate specific behavioural expectations.\\nDefine a review period and consequences.\\nProtect employees from retaliation.\\nPrepare a succession or business-continuity plan.\\nDo not confuse protecting a valuable employee with protecting unacceptable behaviour.\"},{\"step_number\":6,\"key\":\"internalize\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"principle\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":\"No individual should become more important than the organisational standards they are expected to uphold.\",\"reference_example\":\"No individual should become more important than the organisational standards they are expected to uphold.\"}],\"closing_reflection\":{\"prompt\":\"What message does an organisation send when it rewards results but repeatedly ignores the behaviour through which those results are achieved?\",\"journal_input\":{\"field_key\":\"journal\",\"prompt\":\"Optional reflection entry — take a moment to reflect on today\'s scenario.\",\"required\":false}}}', '2026-11-10 12:00:00', '2026-11-10 12:00:00')
