hy?\",\"input_type\":\"single_choice_with_multi_notes\",\"options\":[{\"key\":\"mind_reading\",\"label\":\"Mind Reading\",\"definition\":\"Assuming others\' intentions without evidence.\"},{\"key\":\"fortune_telling\",\"label\":\"Fortune Telling\",\"definition\":\"Treating an unverified prediction as certain.\"},{\"key\":\"emotional_reasoning\",\"label\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"definition\":\"Treating a feeling as proof of reality.\"},{\"key\":\"catastrophizing\",\"label\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"definition\":\"Assuming the absolute worst outcome.\"},{\"key\":\"confirmation_bias\",\"label\":\"Confirmation Bias\",\"definition\":\"Only seeing details that confirm the feared story.\"},{\"key\":\"rumination\",\"label\":\"Rumination\",\"definition\":\"Repetitive, circular overthinking of past events.\"},{\"key\":\"all_or_nothing\",\"label\":\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"definition\":\"Treating a minor gap as total failure or framing as either-or.\"},{\"key\":\"validation_seeking\",\"label\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"definition\":\"Allowing external approval to define self-worth or decisions.\"},{\"key\":\"sunk_cost_fallacy\",\"label\":\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"definition\":\"Continuing a path solely because of past unrecoverable investment.\"}],\"correct_option_key\":\"all_or_nothing\",\"primary_trap\":\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Confirmation Bias\",\"Fortune Telling\",\"Validation Seeking\"],\"secondary_trap_keys\":[\"confirmation_bias\",\"fortune_telling\",\"validation_seeking\"],\"explanation_reveal\":\"after_commit\",\"explanation\":\"Primary Thinking Trap: All-or-Nothing Thinking\\nManagement may treat cost reduction and service quality as opposing choices, overlooking opportunities to improve processes without compromising essential standards.\\nSecondary Thinking Traps:\\nConfirmation Bias: Supporting reductions that achieve financial targets while overlooking indirect costs.\\nFortune Telling: Assuming proposed savings will materialise without negative consequences.\\nValidation Seeking: Prioritising board approval over a balanced operational assessment.\"},{\"step_number\":3,\"key\":\"reality_check\",\"prompt\":\"Answer these questions before deciding what the situation means:\\n• What information would help identify genuine inefficiencies rather than essential operating expenditure?\\n• How could the organisation measure the indirect cost of reducing staffing, training and maintenance?\",\"tip\":\"Missing information is not evidence. Identify what you would need to know before becoming more certain.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What information would help identify genuine inefficiencies rather than essential operating expenditure?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. How could the organisation measure the indirect cost of reducing staffing, training and maintenance?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Department-wise cost structures.\",\"Revenue and productivity per employee.\",\"Overtime and absenteeism.\",\"Service turnaround times.\",\"Customer complaints.\",\"Rework and error rates.\",\"Preventive versus corrective maintenance costs.\",\"Training effectiveness.\",\"Vendor performance.\",\"Long-term replacement and repair costs.\",\"The lowest immediate expense is not necessarily the lowest total cost.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Department-wise cost structures.\",\"Revenue and productivity per employee.\",\"Overtime and absenteeism.\",\"Service turnaround times.\",\"Customer complaints.\",\"Rework and error rates.\",\"Preventive versus corrective maintenance costs.\",\"Training effectiveness.\",\"Vendor performance.\",\"Long-term replacement and repair costs.\",\"The lowest immediate expense is not necessarily the lowest total cost.\"]},{\"step_number\":4,\"key\":\"reframe\",\"prompt\":\"Explore other explanations and alternatives:\\n• What alternatives exist beyond reducing employee strength and cutting operational budgets?\\n• How could you achieve sustainable savings without compromising essential service standards?\",\"insight\":\"Reframing does not mean forcing a positive interpretation. It means expanding the set of explanations or options before deciding.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What alternatives exist beyond reducing employee strength and cutting operational budgets?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. How could you achieve sustainable savings without compromising essential service standards?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Improve workforce scheduling.\",\"Reduce unnecessary overtime.\",\"Renegotiate vendor contracts.\",\"Eliminate process duplication.\",\"Reduce material wastage.\",\"Improve inventory management.\",\"Automate repetitive administrative work.\",\"Improve collection efficiency.\",\"Introduce departmental productivity benchmarks.\",\"Protect critical training and preventive maintenance.\",\"Cost optimisation should improve how resources are used, not simply reduce the resources available.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Improve workforce scheduling.\",\"Reduce unnecessary overtime.\",\"Renegotiate vendor contracts.\",\"Eliminate process duplication.\",\"Reduce material wastage.\",\"Improve inventory management.\",\"Automate repetitive administrative work.\",\"Improve collection efficiency.\",\"Introduce departmental productivity benchmarks.\",\"Protect critical training and preventive maintenance.\",\"Cost optimisation should improve how resources are used, not simply reduce the resources available.\"]},{\"step_number\":5,\"key\":\"intervention\",\"prompt\":\"What would your 90-day cost-optimisation plan prioritise?\",\"reminder\":\"There isn\'t always a perfect action. There is usually a reasonable next action based on the evidence available.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"action\",\"prompt\":\"What would your 90-day cost-optimisation plan prioritise?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Establishing a reliable cost baseline.\",\"Categorising expenses into essential, discretionary and inefficient.\",\"Identifying quick wins.\",\"Assigning departmental savings targets.\",\"Monitoring service quality alongside financial savings.\",\"Testing significant changes before full implementation.\",\"Reviewing actual savings against projections.\",\"Every cost-reduction initiative should have both a financial target and a quality safeguard.\"],\"reference_example\":\"Establishing a reliable cost baseline.\\nCategorising expenses into essential, discretionary and inefficient.\\nIdentifying quick wins.\\nAssigning departmental savings targets.\\nMonitoring service quality alongside financial savings.\\nTesting significant changes before full implementation.\\nReviewing actual savings against projections.\\nEvery cost-reduction initiative should have both a financial target and a quality safeguard.\"},{\"step_number\":6,\"key\":\"internalize\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"principle\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":\"Financial discipline is not about spending the least possible amount. It is about obtaining the best sustainable outcome from available resources.\",\"reference_example\":\"Financial discipline is not about spending the least possible amount. It is about obtaining the best sustainable outcome from available resources.\"}],\"closing_reflection\":{\"prompt\":\"If a cost reduction improves this quarter\'s profitability but creates larger operational expenses next year, was it really a saving?\",\"journal_input\":{\"field_key\":\"journal\",\"prompt\":\"Optional reflection entry — take a moment to reflect on today\'s scenario.\",\"required\":false}}}', `updated_at` = '2026-11-10 12:00:00';

INSERT INTO `foundation_days` (`day_number`, `phase`, `title`, `domain`, `primary_trap`, `secondary_trap`, `content_bundle`, `created_at`, `updated_at`)
VALUES (59, 3, 'The Leadership Decision That Will Make Someone Unhappy', 'Workplace / Organisational Leadership / Conflict Resolution', 'All-or-Nothing Thinking', 'Mind Reading, Validation Seeking, Catastrophizing', '{\"day\":59,\"phase\":3,\"title\":\"The Leadership Decision That Will Make Someone Unhappy\",\"domain\":\"Workplace / Organisational Leadership / Conflict Resolution\",\"mission\":\"Leadership is not the ability to keep everyone satisfied. It is the ability to make defensible decisions despite competing interests.\",\"learning_objective\":\"Evaluate competing stakeholder interests, distinguish popularity from fairness and make difficult organisational decisions with clarity.\",\"difficulty\":\"Advanced\",\"emotional_intensity\":\"High\",\"estimated_minutes\":\"6-8\",\"primary_skill\":\"Evaluate\",\"primary_trap\":\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"secondary_trap\":\"Mind Reading, Validation Seeking, Catastrophizing\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Mind Reading\",\"Validation Seeking\",\"Catastrophizing\"],\"guidance_level\":\"Moderate to high guidance; strategic leadership, financial risk and long-term consequences.\",\"interaction_principle\":\"Think → Commit → Compare → Reflect\",\"scenario_text\":\"You are the CEO of a growing organisation.\\nTwo senior department heads have been working together for several years.\\nBoth have contributed significantly to organisational growth.\\nHowever, a serious conflict has developed between them.\\nThe operations head believes the sales department is making commitments that cannot be delivered.\\nThe sales head believes operations is unnecessarily restrictive and is causing the organisation to lose business.\\nThe disagreement has now reached the employee level:\\nTeams are blaming one another.\\nJoint projects are delayed.\\nCustomer complaints are increasing.\\nSenior management meetings have become confrontational.\\nYou have received separate complaints from both department heads.\\nEach claims to be protecting the organisation\'s interests.\\nBoth are influential, and replacing either would be difficult.\\nYour board wants the conflict resolved quickly.\\nYou must decide whether to mediate, restructure reporting relationships, change leadership responsibilities or take formal action against one or both managers.\",\"steps\":[{\"step_number\":1,\"key\":\"detect\",\"prompt\":\"What are the confirmed facts, and what are you assuming about the intentions and responsibilities of each department head?\",\"insight\":\"Facts describe what is known. Assumptions explain what the mind projects or fears.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"facts\",\"prompt\":\"Write only the confirmed facts.\",\"multiline\":true},{\"field_key\":\"assumptions\",\"prompt\":\"Write what you are assuming about this situation.\",\"multiline\":true}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":{\"facts\":[\"Two senior managers are in conflict.\",\"Their teams are becoming divided.\",\"Joint projects are being delayed.\",\"Customer complaints are increasing.\",\"Both managers claim to represent organisational interests.\"],\"assumptions\":[\"One department is entirely responsible.\",\"One manager is deliberately obstructing the other.\",\"Replacing either individual will resolve the conflict.\",\"Mediation alone will solve the underlying problem.\",\"The conflict may be personal, structural or a combination of both.\"]},\"reference_example\":\"Confirmed facts: Two senior managers are in conflict.; Their teams are becoming divided.; Joint projects are being delayed.\\nAssumptions: One department is entirely responsible.; One manager is deliberately obstructing the other.; Replacing either individual will resolve the conflict.\"},{\"step_number\":2,\"key\":\"decode\",\"prompt\":\"Which thinking trap(s) may be influencing your judgment, and why?\",\"input_type\":\"single_choice_with_multi_notes\",\"options\":[{\"key\":\"mind_reading\",\"label\":\"Mind Reading\",\"definition\":\"Assuming others\' intentions without evidence.\"},{\"key\":\"fortune_telling\",\"label\":\"Fortune Telling\",\"definition\":\"Treating an unverified prediction as certain.\"},{\"key\":\"emotional_reasoning\",\"label\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"definition\":\"Treating a feeling as proof of reality.\"},{\"key\":\"catastrophizing\",\"label\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"definition\":\"Assuming the absolute worst outcome.\"},{\"key\":\"confirmation_bias\",\"label\":\"Confirmation Bias\",\"definition\":\"Only seeing details that confirm the feared story.\"},{\"key\":\"rumination\",\"label\":\"Rumination\",\"definition\":\"Repetitive, circular overthinking of past events.\"},{\"key\":\"all_or_nothing\",\"label\":\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"definition\":\"Treating a minor gap as total failure or framing as either-or.\"},{\"key\":\"validation_seeking\",\"label\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"definition\":\"Allowing external approval to define self-worth or decisions.\"},{\"key\":\"sunk_cost_fallacy\",\"label\":\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"definition\":\"Continuing a path solely because of past unrecoverable investment.\"}],\"correct_option_key\":\"all_or_nothing\",\"primary_trap\":\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Mind Reading\",\"Validation Seeking\",\"Catastrophizing\"],\"secondary_trap_keys\":[\"mind_reading\",\"validation_seeking\",\"catastrophizing\"],\"explanation_reveal\":\"after_commit\",\"explanation\":\"Primary Thinking Trap: All-or-Nothing Thinking\\nThe CEO may believe one department must be right and the other wrong, overlooking shared accountability and structural problems.\\nSecondary Thinking Traps:\\nMind Reading: Assuming the intentions behind each manager\'s actions.\\nValidation Seeking: Avoiding an unpopular decision to retain the approval of both managers.\\nCatastrophizing: Assuming that taking a firm position will cause organisational disruption.\"},{\"step_number\":3,\"key\":\"reality_check\",\"prompt\":\"Answer these questions before deciding what the situation means:\\n• What evidence would help establish the actual causes of the conflict?\\n• Which organisational systems may be contributing to the disagreement?\",\"tip\":\"Missing information is not evidence. Identify what you would need to know before becoming more certain.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What evidence would help establish the actual causes of the conflict?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. Which organisational systems may be contributing to the disagreement?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Sales commitments versus delivery capacity.\",\"Customer complaints and delays.\",\"Departmental performance indicators.\",\"Reporting and escalation structures.\",\"Incentive and compensation arrangements.\",\"Interdepartmental communication records.\",\"Decision-making authority.\",\"Previous attempts at resolution.\",\"A conflict between individuals may sometimes be a symptom of poorly designed organisational systems.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Sales commitments versus delivery capacity.\",\"Customer complaints and delays.\",\"Departmental performance indicators.\",\"Reporting and escalation structures.\",\"Incentive and compensation arrangements.\",\"Interdepartmental communication records.\",\"Decision-making authority.\",\"Previous attempts at resolution.\",\"A conflict between individuals may sometimes be a symptom of poorly designed organisational systems.\"]},{\"step_number\":4,\"key\":\"reframe\",\"prompt\":\"Explore other explanations and alternatives:\\n• What alternatives exist beyond supporting one manager or removing one of them?\\n• How could you redesign responsibilities and incentives to reduce recurring conflict?\",\"insight\":\"Reframing does not mean forcing a positive interpretation. It means expanding the set of explanations or options before deciding.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What alternatives exist beyond supporting one manager or removing one of them?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. How could you redesign responsibilities and incentives to reduce recurring conflict?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Conduct a structured mediation.\",\"Establish shared organisational targets.\",\"Redefine decision-making authority.\",\"Introduce joint planning and review meetings.\",\"Align incentives across departments.\",\"Establish service-level commitments.\",\"Restructure reporting relationships.\",\"Initiate formal performance action if conduct remains unacceptable.\",\"The solution should address the underlying causes rather than merely suppress visible disagreement.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Conduct a structured mediation.\",\"Establish shared organisational targets.\",\"Redefine decision-making authority.\",\"Introduce joint planning and review meetings.\",\"Align incentives across departments.\",\"Establish service-level commitments.\",\"Restructure reporting relationships.\",\"Initiate formal performance action if conduct remains unacceptable.\",\"The solution should address the underlying causes rather than merely suppress visible disagreement.\"]},{\"step_number\":5,\"key\":\"intervention\",\"prompt\":\"What decision would you take, and how would you communicate it to both managers?\",\"reminder\":\"There isn\'t always a perfect action. There is usually a reasonable next action based on the evidence available.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"action\",\"prompt\":\"What decision would you take, and how would you communicate it to both managers?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"A responsible approach would include:\",\"Meeting both managers.\",\"Reviewing documented evidence.\",\"Establishing shared performance expectations.\",\"Clarifying responsibilities and authority.\",\"Setting measurable interdepartmental targets.\",\"Monitoring progress.\",\"Taking formal action if either manager fails to meet expectations.\",\"A CEO must protect organisational performance even when the decision disappoints influential individuals.\"],\"reference_example\":\"A responsible approach would include:\\nMeeting both managers.\\nReviewing documented evidence.\\nEstablishing shared performance expectations.\\nClarifying responsibilities and authority.\\nSetting measurable interdepartmental targets.\\nMonitoring progress.\\nTaking formal action if either manager fails to meet expectations.\\nA CEO must protect organisational performance even when the decision disappoints influential individuals.\"},{\"step_number\":6,\"key\":\"internalize\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"principle\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":\"Fair leadership does not mean satisfying every stakeholder. It means making decisions based on evidence, responsibility and organisational priorities.\",\"reference_example\":\"Fair leadership does not mean satisfying every stakeholder. It means making decisions based on evidence, responsibility and organisational priorities.\"}],\"closing_reflection\":{\"prompt\":\"Are you avoiding a difficult leadership decision because it is genuinely premature, or because you do not want anyone to be unhappy with you?\",\"journal_input\":{\"field_key\":\"journal\",\"prompt\":\"Optional reflection entry — take a moment to reflect on today\'s scenario.\",\"required\":false}}}', '2026-11-10 12:00:00', '2026-11-10 12:00:00')
