,{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. How could you redesign responsibilities and incentives to reduce recurring conflict?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Conduct a structured mediation.\",\"Establish shared organisational targets.\",\"Redefine decision-making authority.\",\"Introduce joint planning and review meetings.\",\"Align incentives across departments.\",\"Establish service-level commitments.\",\"Restructure reporting relationships.\",\"Initiate formal performance action if conduct remains unacceptable.\",\"The solution should address the underlying causes rather than merely suppress visible disagreement.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Conduct a structured mediation.\",\"Establish shared organisational targets.\",\"Redefine decision-making authority.\",\"Introduce joint planning and review meetings.\",\"Align incentives across departments.\",\"Establish service-level commitments.\",\"Restructure reporting relationships.\",\"Initiate formal performance action if conduct remains unacceptable.\",\"The solution should address the underlying causes rather than merely suppress visible disagreement.\"]},{\"step_number\":5,\"key\":\"intervention\",\"prompt\":\"What decision would you take, and how would you communicate it to both managers?\",\"reminder\":\"There isn\'t always a perfect action. There is usually a reasonable next action based on the evidence available.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"action\",\"prompt\":\"What decision would you take, and how would you communicate it to both managers?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"A responsible approach would include:\",\"Meeting both managers.\",\"Reviewing documented evidence.\",\"Establishing shared performance expectations.\",\"Clarifying responsibilities and authority.\",\"Setting measurable interdepartmental targets.\",\"Monitoring progress.\",\"Taking formal action if either manager fails to meet expectations.\",\"A CEO must protect organisational performance even when the decision disappoints influential individuals.\"],\"reference_example\":\"A responsible approach would include:\\nMeeting both managers.\\nReviewing documented evidence.\\nEstablishing shared performance expectations.\\nClarifying responsibilities and authority.\\nSetting measurable interdepartmental targets.\\nMonitoring progress.\\nTaking formal action if either manager fails to meet expectations.\\nA CEO must protect organisational performance even when the decision disappoints influential individuals.\"},{\"step_number\":6,\"key\":\"internalize\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"principle\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":\"Fair leadership does not mean satisfying every stakeholder. It means making decisions based on evidence, responsibility and organisational priorities.\",\"reference_example\":\"Fair leadership does not mean satisfying every stakeholder. It means making decisions based on evidence, responsibility and organisational priorities.\"}],\"closing_reflection\":{\"prompt\":\"Are you avoiding a difficult leadership decision because it is genuinely premature, or because you do not want anyone to be unhappy with you?\",\"journal_input\":{\"field_key\":\"journal\",\"prompt\":\"Optional reflection entry — take a moment to reflect on today\'s scenario.\",\"required\":false}}}', `updated_at` = '2026-11-10 12:00:00';

INSERT INTO `foundation_days` (`day_number`, `phase`, `title`, `domain`, `primary_trap`, `secondary_trap`, `content_bundle`, `created_at`, `updated_at`)
VALUES (60, 3, 'The Problem Everyone Knows About but Nobody Wants to Address', 'Entrepreneurship / Workplace / Strategic Leadership', 'Status Quo Bias', 'Catastrophizing, Validation Seeking, Sunk Cost Fallacy', '{\"day\":60,\"phase\":3,\"title\":\"The Problem Everyone Knows About but Nobody Wants to Address\",\"domain\":\"Entrepreneurship / Workplace / Strategic Leadership\",\"mission\":\"Avoiding a difficult decision is still a decision, and its consequences may become more expensive with time.\",\"learning_objective\":\"Recognise the cost of inaction, identify organisational avoidance and evaluate when delayed intervention becomes a leadership failure.\",\"difficulty\":\"Advanced\",\"emotional_intensity\":\"High\",\"estimated_minutes\":\"6-8\",\"primary_skill\":\"Evaluate\",\"primary_trap\":\"Status Quo Bias\",\"secondary_trap\":\"Catastrophizing, Validation Seeking, Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Catastrophizing\",\"Validation Seeking\",\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\"],\"guidance_level\":\"Moderate to high guidance; strategic leadership, financial risk and long-term consequences.\",\"interaction_principle\":\"Think → Commit → Compare → Reflect\",\"scenario_text\":\"You have recently taken charge of a business that has been operating for more than 15 years.\\nThe organisation has an established customer base and a relatively experienced management team.\\nDuring your first few months, you notice a recurring problem.\\nOne of the organisation\'s major operating processes is inefficient.\\nThe problem has existed for several years:\\nEmployees regularly bypass established procedures.\\nManagers use informal arrangements to complete routine work.\\nImportant records are maintained inconsistently.\\nSome financial discrepancies remain unexplained.\\nCustomer complaints have increased.\\nPrevious management teams have discussed the issue but never implemented a permanent solution.\\nThe employees responsible for the process argue that the current system is imperfect but functional.\\nThe department head believes that introducing stricter controls will slow operations.\\nThe finance team wants immediate corrective action.\\nYou are also managing several other priorities, including business growth, recruitment and cost optimisation.\\nTaking action could create resistance among experienced employees and temporarily disrupt operations.\\nHowever, continuing with the existing arrangement could expose the organisation to larger financial, operational and reputational risks.\\nYou must decide whether to intervene immediately, investigate further, introduce gradual changes or continue monitoring the situation.\",\"steps\":[{\"step_number\":1,\"key\":\"detect\",\"prompt\":\"What are the confirmed facts, and what are you assuming about the consequences of taking action or continuing with the existing process?\",\"insight\":\"Facts describe what is known. Assumptions explain what the mind projects or fears.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"facts\",\"prompt\":\"Write only the confirmed facts.\",\"multiline\":true},{\"field_key\":\"assumptions\",\"prompt\":\"Write what you are assuming about this situation.\",\"multiline\":true}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":{\"facts\":[\"The process has been inefficient for several years.\",\"Employees routinely bypass established procedures.\",\"Records are inconsistently maintained.\",\"Financial discrepancies remain unexplained.\",\"Customer complaints have increased.\",\"Previous management teams have failed to implement permanent corrective action.\"],\"assumptions\":[\"The current system is manageable because it has existed for years.\",\"Employees will resist any meaningful change.\",\"Correcting the problem will disrupt operations.\",\"The problem can be addressed later without significant consequences.\",\"The duration of a problem does not make it acceptable or safe.\"]},\"reference_example\":\"Confirmed facts: The process has been inefficient for several years.; Employees routinely bypass established procedures.; Records are inconsistently maintained.\\nAssumptions: The current system is manageable because it has existed for years.; Employees will resist any meaningful change.; Correcting the problem will disrupt operations.\"},{\"step_number\":2,\"key\":\"decode\",\"prompt\":\"Which thinking trap(s) may be influencing your judgment, and why?\",\"input_type\":\"single_choice_with_multi_notes\",\"options\":[{\"key\":\"mind_reading\",\"label\":\"Mind Reading\",\"definition\":\"Assuming others\' intentions without evidence.\"},{\"key\":\"fortune_telling\",\"label\":\"Fortune Telling\",\"definition\":\"Treating an unverified prediction as certain.\"},{\"key\":\"emotional_reasoning\",\"label\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"definition\":\"Treating a feeling as proof of reality.\"},{\"key\":\"catastrophizing\",\"label\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"definition\":\"Assuming the absolute worst outcome.\"},{\"key\":\"confirmation_bias\",\"label\":\"Confirmation Bias\",\"definition\":\"Only seeing details that confirm the feared story.\"},{\"key\":\"rumination\",\"label\":\"Rumination\",\"definition\":\"Repetitive, circular overthinking of past events.\"},{\"key\":\"all_or_nothing\",\"label\":\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"definition\":\"Treating a minor gap as total failure or framing as either-or.\"},{\"key\":\"validation_seeking\",\"label\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"definition\":\"Allowing external approval to define self-worth or decisions.\"},{\"key\":\"sunk_cost_fallacy\",\"label\":\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"definition\":\"Continuing a path solely because of past unrecoverable investment.\"},{\"key\":\"status_quo_bias\",\"label\":\"Status Quo Bias\",\"definition\":\"Preferring things to remain the same or tolerating an inefficient process because it is familiar.\"}],\"correct_option_key\":\"status_quo_bias\",\"primary_trap\":\"Status Quo Bias\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Catastrophizing\",\"Validation Seeking\",\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\"],\"secondary_trap_keys\":[\"catastrophizing\",\"validation_seeking\",\"sunk_cost_fallacy\"],\"explanation_reveal\":\"after_commit\",\"explanation\":\"Primary Thinking Trap: Status Quo Bias\\nThe leader may tolerate an inefficient process because it is familiar and has existed for a long time.\\nSecondary Thinking Traps:\\nCatastrophizing: Assuming that introducing change will create serious operational disruption.\\nValidation Seeking: Avoiding action to maintain the approval of experienced employees.\\nSunk Cost Fallacy: Preserving an established arrangement because considerable time and effort have already been invested in it.\\nInaction can appear less risky because its immediate consequences are less visible.\"},{\"step_number\":3,\"key\":\"reality_check\",\"prompt\":\"Answer these questions before deciding what the situation means:\\n• What information would help determine the actual severity and urgency of the problem?\\n• What could happen if the organisation continues operating this way for another 12 months?\",\"tip\":\"Missing information is not evidence. Identify what you would need to know before becoming more certain.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What information would help determine the actual severity and urgency of the problem?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. What could happen if the organisation continues operating this way for another 12 months?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Financial discrepancies and audit findings.\",\"Process failure frequency.\",\"Customer complaints.\",\"Employee workarounds.\",\"Regulatory and contractual obligations.\",\"Existing internal controls.\",\"Potential financial exposure.\",\"Operational dependency on individual employees.\",\"Cost and time required for corrective action.\",\"The risk of inaction must be compared with the risk of intervention.\",\"A process that appears operationally functional may still contain serious control weaknesses.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Financial discrepancies and audit findings.\",\"Process failure frequency.\",\"Customer complaints.\",\"Employee workarounds.\",\"Regulatory and contractual obligations.\",\"Existing internal controls.\",\"Potential financial exposure.\",\"Operational dependency on individual employees.\",\"Cost and time required for corrective action.\",\"The risk of inaction must be compared with the risk of intervention.\",\"A process that appears operationally functional may still contain serious control weaknesses.\"]},{\"step_number\":4,\"key\":\"reframe\",\"prompt\":\"Explore other explanations and alternatives:\\n• What alternatives exist beyond leaving the process unchanged or immediately implementing a complete restructuring?\\n• How could you introduce corrective measures without unnecessarily destabilising the organisation?\",\"insight\":\"Reframing does not mean forcing a positive interpretation. It means expanding the set of explanations or options before deciding.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What alternatives exist beyond leaving the process unchanged or immediately implementing a complete restructuring?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. How could you introduce corrective measures without unnecessarily destabilising the organisation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Conduct an independent process audit.\",\"Identify the highest-risk control failures.\",\"Introduce immediate safeguards for critical risks.\",\"Pilot a revised workflow.\",\"Involve experienced employees in redesigning the process.\",\"Establish phased implementation.\",\"Introduce regular compliance monitoring.\",\"Assign clear process ownership.\",\"Set measurable deadlines for corrective action.\",\"Not every change needs to happen simultaneously. However, critical risks should not be left untreated merely because the larger transformation takes time.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Conduct an independent process audit.\",\"Identify the highest-risk control failures.\",\"Introduce immediate safeguards for critical risks.\",\"Pilot a revised workflow.\",\"Involve experienced employees in redesigning the process.\",\"Establish phased implementation.\",\"Introduce regular compliance monitoring.\",\"Assign clear process ownership.\",\"Set measurable deadlines for corrective action.\",\"Not every change needs to happen simultaneously. However, critical risks should not be left untreated merely because the larger transformation takes time.\"]},{\"step_number\":5,\"key\":\"intervention\",\"prompt\":\"What would you do in the first 30 days, and how would you ensure that the issue is finally resolved?\",\"reminder\":\"There isn\'t always a perfect action. There is usually a reasonable next action based on the evidence available.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"action\",\"prompt\":\"What would you do in the first 30 days, and how would you ensure that the issue is finally resolved?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Establishing the facts through an independent review.\",\"Identifying immediate financial and operational risks.\",\"Introducing temporary safeguards where necessary.\",\"Assigning a responsible process owner.\",\"Preparing a corrective action plan.\",\"Establishing deadlines and measurable milestones.\",\"Monitoring implementation.\",\"Escalating unresolved issues to senior management or the board where appropriate.\",\"A CEO should not confuse maintaining operational continuity with tolerating persistent operational weaknesses.\"],\"reference_example\":\"Establishing the facts through an independent review.\\nIdentifying immediate financial and operational risks.\\nIntroducing temporary safeguards where necessary.\\nAssigning a responsible process owner.\\nPreparing a corrective action plan.\\nEstablishing deadlines and measurable milestones.\\nMonitoring implementation.\\nEscalating unresolved issues to senior management or the board where appropriate.\\nA CEO should not confuse maintaining operational continuity with tolerating persistent operational weaknesses.\"},{\"step_number\":6,\"key\":\"internalize\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"principle\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":\"Avoiding a difficult decision does not eliminate its consequences. In many situations, delayed action allows manageable problems to become serious organisational failures.\",\"reference_example\":\"Avoiding a difficult decision does not eliminate its consequences. In many situations, delayed action allows manageable problems to become serious organisational failures.\"}],\"closing_reflection\":{\"prompt\":\"If you already know that a problem is damaging your organisation, what additional evidence are you waiting for, and is that delay genuinely justified?\",\"journal_input\":{\"field_key\":\"journal\",\"prompt\":\"Optional reflection entry — take a moment to reflect on today\'s scenario.\",\"required\":false}}}', '2026-11-10 12:00:00', '2026-11-10 12:00:00')
ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE `title` = 'The Problem Everyone Knows About but Nobody Wants to Address', `domain` = 'Entrepreneurship / Workplace / Strategic Leadership', `primary_trap` = 'Status Quo Bias', `secondary_trap` = 'Catastrophizing, Validation Seeking, Sunk Cost Fallacy', `content_bundle` = '{\"day\":60,\"phase\":3,\"title\":\"The Problem Everyone Knows About but Nobody Wants to Address\",\"domain\":\"Entrepreneurship / Workplace / Strategic Leadership\",\"mission\":\"Avoiding a difficult decision is still a decision, and its consequences may become more expensive with time.\",\"learning_objective\":\"Recognise the cost of inaction, identify organisational avoidance and evaluate when delayed intervention becomes a leadership failure.\",\"difficulty\":\"Advanced\",\"emotional_intensity\":\"High\",\"estimated_minutes\":\"6-8\",\"primary_skill\":\"Evaluate\",\"primary_trap\":\"Status Quo Bias\",\"secondary_trap\":\"Catastrophizing, Validation Seeking, Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Catastrophizing\",\"Validation Seeking\",\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\"],\"guidance_level\":\"Moderate to high guidance; strategic leadership, financial risk and long-term consequences.\",\"interaction_principle\":\"Think → Commit → Compare → Reflect\",\"scenario_text\":\"You have recently taken charge of a business that has been operating for more than 15 years.\\nThe organisation has an established customer base and a relatively experienced management team.\\nDuring your first few months, you notice a recurring problem.\\nOne of the organisation\'s major operating processes is inefficient.\\nThe problem has existed for several years:\\nEmployees regularly bypass established procedures.\\nManagers use informal arrangements to complete routine work.\\nImportant records are maintained inconsistently.\\nSome financial discrepancies remain unexplained.\\nCustomer complaints have increased.\\nPrevious management teams have discussed the issue but never implemented a permanent solution.\\nThe employees responsible for the process argue that the current system is imperfect but functional.\\nThe department head believes that introducing stricter controls will slow operations.\\nThe finance team wants immediate corrective action.\\nYou are also managing several other priorities, including business growth, recruitment and cost optimisation.\\nTaking action could create resistance among experienced employees and temporarily disrupt operations.\\nHowever, continuing with the existing arrangement could expose the organisation to larger financial, operational and reputational risks.\\nYou must decide whether to intervene immediately, investigate further, introduce gradual changes or continue monitoring the situation.\",\"steps\":[{\"step_number\":1,\"key\":\"detect\",\"prompt\":\"What are the confirmed facts, and what are you assuming about the consequences of taking action or continuing with the existing process?\",\"insight\":\"Facts describe what is known. Assumptions explain what the mind projects or fears.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"facts\",\"prompt\":\"Write only the confirmed facts.\",\"multiline\":true},{\"field_key\":\"assumptions\",\"prompt\":\"Write what you are assuming about this situation.\",\"multiline\":true}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":{\"facts\":[\"The process has been inefficient for several years.\",\"Employees routinely bypass established procedures.\",\"Records are inconsistently maintained.\",\"Financial discrepancies remain unexplained.\",\"Customer complaints have increased.\",\"Previous management teams have failed to implement permanent corrective action.\"],\"assumptions\":[\"The current system is manageable because it has existed for years.\",\"Employees will resist any meaningful change.\",\"Correcting the problem will disrupt operations.\",\"The problem can be addressed later without significant consequences.\",\"The duration of a problem does not make it acceptable or safe.\"]},\"reference_example\":\"Confirmed facts: The process has been inefficient for several years.; Employees routinely bypass established procedures.; Records are inconsistently maintained.\\nAssumptions: The current system is manageable because it has existed for years.; Employees will resist any meaningful change.; Correcting the problem will disrupt operations.\"},{\"step_number\":2,\"key\":\"decode\",\"prompt\":\"Which thinking trap(s) may be influencing your judgment, and why?\",\"input_type\":\"single_choice_with_multi_notes\",\"options\":[{\"key\":\"mind_reading\",\"label\":\"Mind Reading\",\"definition\":\"Assuming others\' intentions without evidence.\"},{\"key\":\"fortune_telling\",\"label\":\"Fortune Telling\",\"definition\":\"Treating an unverified prediction as certain.\"},{\"key\":\"emotional_reasoning\",\"label\":\"Emotional Reasoning\",\"definition\":\"Treating a feeling as proof of reality.\"},{\"key\":\"catastrophizing\",\"label\":\"Catastrophizing\",\"definition\":\"Assuming the absolute worst outcome.\"},{\"key\":\"confirmation_bias\",\"label\":\"Confirmation Bias\",\"definition\":\"Only seeing details that confirm the feared story.\"},{\"key\":\"rumination\",\"label\":\"Rumination\",\"definition\":\"Repetitive, circular overthinking of past events.\"},{\"key\":\"all_or_nothing\",\"label\":\"All-or-Nothing Thinking\",\"definition\":\"Treating a minor gap as total failure or framing as either-or.\"},{\"key\":\"validation_seeking\",\"label\":\"Validation Seeking\",\"definition\":\"Allowing external approval to define self-worth or decisions.\"},{\"key\":\"sunk_cost_fallacy\",\"label\":\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\",\"definition\":\"Continuing a path solely because of past unrecoverable investment.\"},{\"key\":\"status_quo_bias\",\"label\":\"Status Quo Bias\",\"definition\":\"Preferring things to remain the same or tolerating an inefficient process because it is familiar.\"}],\"correct_option_key\":\"status_quo_bias\",\"primary_trap\":\"Status Quo Bias\",\"secondary_traps\":[\"Catastrophizing\",\"Validation Seeking\",\"Sunk Cost Fallacy\"],\"secondary_trap_keys\":[\"catastrophizing\",\"validation_seeking\",\"sunk_cost_fallacy\"],\"explanation_reveal\":\"after_commit\",\"explanation\":\"Primary Thinking Trap: Status Quo Bias\\nThe leader may tolerate an inefficient process because it is familiar and has existed for a long time.\\nSecondary Thinking Traps:\\nCatastrophizing: Assuming that introducing change will create serious operational disruption.\\nValidation Seeking: Avoiding action to maintain the approval of experienced employees.\\nSunk Cost Fallacy: Preserving an established arrangement because considerable time and effort have already been invested in it.\\nInaction can appear less risky because its immediate consequences are less visible.\"},{\"step_number\":3,\"key\":\"reality_check\",\"prompt\":\"Answer these questions before deciding what the situation means:\\n• What information would help determine the actual severity and urgency of the problem?\\n• What could happen if the organisation continues operating this way for another 12 months?\",\"tip\":\"Missing information is not evidence. Identify what you would need to know before becoming more certain.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What information would help determine the actual severity and urgency of the problem?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. What could happen if the organisation continues operating this way for another 12 months?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Financial discrepancies and audit findings.\",\"Process failure frequency.\",\"Customer complaints.\",\"Employee workarounds.\",\"Regulatory and contractual obligations.\",\"Existing internal controls.\",\"Potential financial exposure.\",\"Operational dependency on individual employees.\",\"Cost and time required for corrective action.\",\"The risk of inaction must be compared with the risk of intervention.\",\"A process that appears operationally functional may still contain serious control weaknesses.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Financial discrepancies and audit findings.\",\"Process failure frequency.\",\"Customer complaints.\",\"Employee workarounds.\",\"Regulatory and contractual obligations.\",\"Existing internal controls.\",\"Potential financial exposure.\",\"Operational dependency on individual employees.\",\"Cost and time required for corrective action.\",\"The risk of inaction must be compared with the risk of intervention.\",\"A process that appears operationally functional may still contain serious control weaknesses.\"]},{\"step_number\":4,\"key\":\"reframe\",\"prompt\":\"Explore other explanations and alternatives:\\n• What alternatives exist beyond leaving the process unchanged or immediately implementing a complete restructuring?\\n• How could you introduce corrective measures without unnecessarily destabilising the organisation?\",\"insight\":\"Reframing does not mean forcing a positive interpretation. It means expanding the set of explanations or options before deciding.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"q1\",\"prompt\":\"1. What alternatives exist beyond leaving the process unchanged or immediately implementing a complete restructuring?\"},{\"field_key\":\"q2\",\"prompt\":\"2. How could you introduce corrective measures without unnecessarily destabilising the organisation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Conduct an independent process audit.\",\"Identify the highest-risk control failures.\",\"Introduce immediate safeguards for critical risks.\",\"Pilot a revised workflow.\",\"Involve experienced employees in redesigning the process.\",\"Establish phased implementation.\",\"Introduce regular compliance monitoring.\",\"Assign clear process ownership.\",\"Set measurable deadlines for corrective action.\",\"Not every change needs to happen simultaneously. However, critical risks should not be left untreated merely because the larger transformation takes time.\"],\"reference_example\":[\"Conduct an independent process audit.\",\"Identify the highest-risk control failures.\",\"Introduce immediate safeguards for critical risks.\",\"Pilot a revised workflow.\",\"Involve experienced employees in redesigning the process.\",\"Establish phased implementation.\",\"Introduce regular compliance monitoring.\",\"Assign clear process ownership.\",\"Set measurable deadlines for corrective action.\",\"Not every change needs to happen simultaneously. However, critical risks should not be left untreated merely because the larger transformation takes time.\"]},{\"step_number\":5,\"key\":\"intervention\",\"prompt\":\"What would you do in the first 30 days, and how would you ensure that the issue is finally resolved?\",\"reminder\":\"There isn\'t always a perfect action. There is usually a reasonable next action based on the evidence available.\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"action\",\"prompt\":\"What would you do in the first 30 days, and how would you ensure that the issue is finally resolved?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":[\"Establishing the facts through an independent review.\",\"Identifying immediate financial and operational risks.\",\"Introducing temporary safeguards where necessary.\",\"Assigning a responsible process owner.\",\"Preparing a corrective action plan.\",\"Establishing deadlines and measurable milestones.\",\"Monitoring implementation.\",\"Escalating unresolved issues to senior management or the board where appropriate.\",\"A CEO should not confuse maintaining operational continuity with tolerating persistent operational weaknesses.\"],\"reference_example\":\"Establishing the facts through an independent review.\\nIdentifying immediate financial and operational risks.\\nIntroducing temporary safeguards where necessary.\\nAssigning a responsible process owner.\\nPreparing a corrective action plan.\\nEstablishing deadlines and measurable milestones.\\nMonitoring implementation.\\nEscalating unresolved issues to senior management or the board where appropriate.\\nA CEO should not confuse maintaining operational continuity with tolerating persistent operational weaknesses.\"},{\"step_number\":6,\"key\":\"internalize\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\",\"inputs\":[{\"field_key\":\"principle\",\"prompt\":\"What judgment principle will you take away from this situation?\"}],\"thinkclear_thinking\":\"Avoiding a difficult decision does not eliminate its consequences. In many situations, delayed action allows manageable problems to become serious organisational failures.\",\"reference_example\":\"Avoiding a difficult decision does not eliminate its consequences. In many situations, delayed action allows manageable problems to become serious organisational failures.\"}],\"closing_reflection\":{\"prompt\":\"If you already know that a problem is damaging your organisation, what additional evidence are you waiting for, and is that delay genuinely justified?\",\"journal_input\":{\"field_key\":\"journal\",\"prompt\":\"Optional reflection entry — take a moment to reflect on today\'s scenario.\",\"required\":false}}}', `updated_at` = '2026-11-10 12:00:00';

